TY  - CONF
TI  - Machine-Learning-Enhanced Process Mining for Anomaly Detection in Cyber-Physical Business Systems
AU  - C. Tian
T2  - 2026 International Conference on Computer Intelligence and Software Engineering (CICSE)
AB  - Cyber-physical business systems (CPBSs) often produce both business-event data and physical-state data. In a real deployment, these streams can be linked only when they share native case identifiers, timestamps, or operational objects. This paper therefore proposes a machine-learning-enhanced process mining framework for such coupled settings, but the public-data evaluation is deliberately redesigned as an independent two-domain benchmark. BPI Challenge 2019 is used only for purchase-order process behavior, and SWaT is used only for water-treatment physical-state behavior. The two datasets are not synchronized at the case level, and no artificial BPI-SWaT case pairs are created. The framework combines conformance diagnostics, temporal-resource features, sequence learning, and activity-conditioned residual modeling. Reported values are macro-averaged across the two independent evaluation tasks rather than interpreted as evidence from one unified business-physical case log. The proposed model achieves 93.4% precision, 91.8% recall, and a 92.6% F1-score under this revised protocol, showing that process-mining evidence and machine-learning evidence are complementary while avoiding unsupported cross-domain linkage.
