TI  - Transformer Models for Automated Business Process Mining and Optimization
AU  - S. Purandare
AU  - C. Kumar Jha
AU  - T. Soundarapandian
AU  - M. Saxena
AU  - V. Brar
AU  - P. Jha
AU  - S. Patsariya
T2  - 2025 3rd World Conference on Communication & Computing (WCONF)
AB  - Business Process Mining (BPM) has emerged as a critical approach for analyzing and optimizing organizational workflows through event log data. Traditional process mining techniques often struggle with long-range dependencies and complex temporal patterns in business processes. This paper presents a novel framework leveraging Transformer-based deep learning models for automated business process mining and optimization. We introduce ProcessTransformer, an attention-based architecture specifically designed to capture intricate process dependencies and predict process outcomes with high accuracy. Our approach incorporates multi-head self-attention mechanisms to model temporal relationships in event sequences and employs positional encoding to preserve sequential information. We evaluate our method on three real-world datasets: BPI Challenge 2012, BPI Challenge 2019, and the Sepsis Cases dataset, achieving superior performance compared to traditional approaches including Long Short-Term Memory networks and Gradient Boosting Machines. Experimental results demonstrate that ProcessTransformer achieves an average F1-score of 0.923, outperforming baseline methods by 12.7% on next-activity prediction tasks and reducing prediction time by 34%. The proposed framework also enables interpretable process insights through attention weight visualization, facilitating data-driven process optimization strategies.
