TY  - CONF
TI  - An Unsupervised Deep Learning Frameworks for Detecting Zero-Day Like Attacks Using System Logs Sequence Anomalies
AU  - E. Ajitha
AU  - Y. M P J
AU  - V. V
AU  - A. Vinora
AU  - J. S. O
T2  - 2026 International Conference on Emerging in Computing Technologies (INCECT)
AB  - The modern computer systems generate a vast number of logs related to events such as the execution of processes, file manipulation, and system errors. These logs play a crucial role in the identification of any anomalies that might point to any cyberattacks. Conventional IDS techniques mainly utilize the signature-based or rules-based approach. The conventional techniques tend to miss out on the detection of zero-day attacks and novel malware. This research paper presents an unsupervised learning paradigm based on deep learning that can help identify anomalies from the sequence of system logs. The data set used in the current study consisted of system log data from the Hadoop Distributed File System (HDFS). First, we performed preprocessing on the log data to transform the data set into numeric values through data cleaning and encoding processes. Then, we created sequential log windows that represent temporal behavior of the systems. The LSTM Autoencoder was used for training the model, which can detect regularity in the sequence. In the process of detecting anomalies through the reconstruction phase, we measure the reconstruction error, which is then used for anomaly detection of log sequences. The proposed model was tested through evaluation of the distribution of reconstruction loss and the use of techniques in visual anomaly detection. The experiment results indicate that the model successfully learns the normal behavior of logs as well as detects anomalous system behaviors. The findings demonstrate the possibility of deep learning approaches in modeling log sequences in anomaly detection. This proposed model provides an automatic solution to analyzing system logs for anomaly detection, and since it does not depend on prior knowledge of the type of attack, it is capable of detecting new types of attacks. This study demonstrates how machine learning algorithms can be used to advance cybersecurity practices through intelligent, data-driven detection systems.
