TI  - Type-Conditioned Heterogeneous Transformer for Unsupervised Anomaly Detection in Object-Centric Event Logs
AU  - Y. Wang
AU  - Y. Jin
AU  - M. Gao
T2  - 2026 6th International Conference on Machine Learning and Intelligent Systems Engineering (MLISE)
AB  - Process mining plays an important role in business process management by enabling process discovery, conformance checking, anomaly detection, and process optimization through the analysis of event logs generated by information systems. Nevertheless, traditional case-centric flat event logs are inherently limited in capturing the complex interactions among multiple objects in real-world processes. Object-centric event logs (OCELs) address this limitation by moving beyond the single-case paradigm and explicitly modeling event-multi-object relationships, thus providing richer data support for the accurate modeling and in-depth analysis of complex business processes. To tackle the challenges that the inherent heterogeneity and complex interaction structures of OCELs present to conventional process mining techniques, and to enhance anomaly detection performance, this paper constructs heterogeneous temporal graphs from OCELs and proposes a novel unsupervised anomaly detection framework, namely the Type-Conditioned Heterogeneous Transformer (TCHT). Specifically, the framework introduces a type-conditioned attention mechanism, virtual type neighbor tokens for local context aggregation, and a gated recurrent unit-based object state update module. The model is optimized through a multitask learning objective that combines inter-node link prediction, masked event modeling, and contrastive learning over state transitions. Experimental results on multiple datasets demonstrate that TCHT outperforms baseline methods across several evaluation metrics, including F1 score, AUC-ROC, AUCPR, and Recall@10.
