AB  - Windows Event Logs serve as a critical telemetry source for detecting malware infections, insider threats, privilege abuse, and system misconfigurations within enterprise environments. However, the sheer volume and complexity of these logs make manual analysis impractical and prone to oversight. This paper presents a real-time, webenabled log analytics and anomaly detection framework that leverages an unsupervised Isolation Forest algorithm for behavioral profiling of Windows Event Logs. The proposed architecture employs a Python-MongoDB-Flask pipeline for continuous log ingestion, schema-flexible storage, and highspeed querying. To address the explainability gap in machine learning-driven security analytics, the system integrates SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations) to attribute anomaly scores to specific log features, enabling transparent, analysttrustworthy decisions. An interactive dashboard delivers dynamic visualizations, temporal trend analysis, and contextual log filtering, empowering analysts to investigate anomalous activities with reduced cognitive load. Emphasizing operational scalability, modularity, and forensic interpretability, the system is designed to support real-time threat hunting and proactive security decision-making in mission-critical enterprise deployments.
