AU  - Y. Lin
T2  - 2025 10th International Conference on Cyber Security and Information Engineering (ICCSIE)
AB  - As computer systems grow in scale and complexity, system logs play a critical role in fault diagnosis, security auditing, and anomaly detection. Traditional methods struggle with high-dimensional, unstructured, and large-scale log data, leading to low accuracy and poor generalization. This paper presents a robust and interpretable anomaly detection model based on ensemble learning, integrating Extreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost), Light Gradient Boosting Machine (LightGBM), and Categorical Boosting (CatBoost) via Bagging and Stacking strategies. To enhance data quality and model stability, preprocessing steps include K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN) based missing value imputation, Interquartile Range (IQR) based outlier removal, and Yeo-Johnson transformation. Model training incorporates RandomizedSearchCV for hyperparameter optimization and Stratified K-Fold for balanced evaluation. Experimental results demonstrate excellent performance in terms of Accuracy, Precision, Recall, and F1 Score, while Receiver Operating Characteristic (ROC) curves and confusion matrices validate the model’s classification ability. Furthermore, SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) analysis provides feature-level interpretability. The proposed approach offers a scalable, accurate, and interpretable solution for intelligent system log anomaly detection.
