TY  - CONF
TI  - Deep Learning—Based Framework for Software Log Analysis and Suspicious Activity Prediction (DL-SLAP)
AU  - P. Rana
AU  - M. Iqbal
AU  - H. Tyagi
T2  - 2026 International Conference on Intelligent Systems in Engineering, Secured Systems and Cybersecurity (ICISESSC)
AB  - The Increasing complexity of software systems and their scale has resulted in the creation of a vast amount of diverse and unique log data. This has made it impossible to use manual monitoring or rule-based procedures to quickly detect suspicious activities. This document will describe a system called DL-SLAP, which has been developed to automate the intelligent, proactive analysis of Software Logs for detecting suspicious activities via Deep Learning techniques. DL-SLAP combines four steps, which are Log Ingestion, Pre-processing, Semantic Feature Extraction and Analysis, and the application of Relevant Deep Learning Techniques, as well as using deep-learning-based features to identify both abnormal behaviors and possible malicious intent in the software's behaviors. The architecture of DL-SLAP is hybrid, containing three components. The first is an LSTM AutoEncoder used for the anomaly detection process; the second is a Bi-directional LSTM (Bi-LSTM) to predict the next sequence in the log; the last is a CNN-LSTM Neural Network that allows for the capturing of log pattern information in both spatial-temporal ways. All three components were developed and tested using publicly-available repositories of System Log data, in contrast to current techniques used by most organizations when using machinelearning techniques. The results of the experiments show that DL-SLAP outperformed previous machine learning techniques and state-of-the-art software Log Analysis techniques. In fact, the results of the experiments shows an increase of $\mathbf{1 2. 8 \%}$ in detection accuracy compared to the baseline LSTM-based models. An increase of $\mathbf{1 5. 4 \%}$ in the F1-Score. A reduction of 18.6 % in the number of false-positive alerts produced. A 22%reduction in latency for generating anomaly alerts from DLSLAP compared to the baseline LSTM models, allowing for nearly real-time alert generation. These results indicate that DL-SLAP offers a superior solution for anomaly detection and alert generation when compared to existing systems.
