AU  - N. Krishna
AU  - G. Paramesh
AU  - K. Kiran Kumar
T2  - 2026 International Conference on AI-Driven Innovations & Applications (IC-AIDA)
AB  - This study presents an operational machinelearning pipeline for detecting lateral movement within Windowsbased enterprise environments using Windows Security Event Logs. We constructed a labeled dataset from a controlled Active Directory testbed (workstations and servers) and engineered 73 per-user behavioral features encompassing temporal patterns, privilege-change events, and inter-host authentication sequences. Our detection ensemble combines Isolation Forest (unsupervised baseline), Random Forest and XGBoost (supervised classifiers), and an LSTM sequence model; ensemble outputs are fused into a weighted risk score mapped to MITRE ATT&CK techniques. In evaluation on the simulated dataset, the ensemble achieved high discriminatory performance (ensemble ROC-AUC 0.98; F1 $\approx 96.4 \%$), improving detection of credential misuse, privilege escalation, and cross-host authentication anomalies while reducing low-value alerts. A Streamlit analyst interface with SHAP-based explanations was implemented to support triage. We emphasize reproducibility: the Methods section documents data collection, feature construction, per-user baseline computation, and model hyperparameters so future work can extend the pipeline to diverse enterprise deployments. (See Methodology for dataset generation and experimental setup details.)
