AU  - S. S
AU  - A. P
T2  - 2026 4th International Conference on Sustainable Computing and Smart Systems (ICSCSS)
AB  - This study presents a deep learning-based approach to cybersecurity threat detection using Windows Sysmon system event logs. The proposed model employs a lightweight one-dimensional convolutional neural network (1D-CNN) architecture with 64 filters, ReLU activation, and progressive dropout regularization to analyse nine features derived from Sysmon logs encompassing process creation, network connections, file operations, and registry modifications. The model was trained and evaluated on a real-world enterprise Sysmon dataset comprising 1,000 annotated records with balanced benign (48%) and malicious (52%) class distribution. Experimental results demonstrate high classification performance with overall accuracy of 97%, precision of 97%, recall of 97%, and F1-score of 97%, outperforming five established baseline classifiers including Logistic Regression, Random Forest, SVM, k-NN, and Naive Bayes by 3-8 percentage points across all metrics. Five-fold cross-validation confirms statistical robustness with mean accuracy of 96.8% and standard deviation below 1%. The lightweight architecture achieves inference times of 0.42 milliseconds per sample, enabling real-time threat detection in enterprise environments.
