TI  - Adaptive Log Anomaly Detection for Networked System in Cold-Start Extreme Verification Latency
AU  - Q. Shi
AU  - Z. Yang
AU  - S. Shao
T2  - 2026 35th International Conference on Computer Communications and Networks (ICCCN)
AB  - System logs are critical for monitoring and securing modern networked systems, including mobile platforms, edge devices, and cloud services. Real-world deployments pose persistent challenges for log anomaly detection (LAD) because labeled data are limited, analyst feedback is delayed or absent, and evolving systems and workloads cause continuous shifts in the distribution. These issues are particularly severe under cold-start extreme verification latency (EVL), where detectors are forced to operate without the labeled initialization set and post-deployment labels. To address the challenge, we propose DAALog, an unsupervised drift-aware adaptive framework for LAD under cold-start EVL. Built on a novel EVL-AT, DAALog enables drift-aware adaptive learning by continuously monitoring distributional changes with an unsupervised concept drift detector, preserving high-confidence normal instances as an adaptive reference set, and retraining the model when significant drift is detected. It further incorporates the EM algorithm to eliminate the need for manual threshold tuning. Experiments on the OpenStack and BGL datasets demonstrate that DAALog consistently achieves more stable detection performance than the non-adaptive baseline, making it suitable for long-running systems where feedback and continuous operator intervention are impractical.
