AU  - S. Alzubari
AU  - S. Khan
T2  - 2025 IEEE International Conference on Emerging Trends in Engineering and Computing (ETECOM)
AB  - The immense volume and semi-structured complexity of Windows Event Logs (WEL) make manual analysis exceptionally difficult, often causing critical delays in incident response and allowing sophisticated cyber threats to go undetected as some anomalies and threats are recorded as benign in WEL like Privilege Escalation attempts and Log Clearing attempts. This research addresses the limitations of manual and conventional analysis by investigating the efficacy of machine learning, particularly Graph Neural Networks (GNNs), for automated anomaly detection. A quantitative experimental methodology was employed, wherein a controlled lab environment was established to simulate three common and critical attack techniques: Brute Force, Privilege Escalation (specifically UAC Bypass), and Log Clearing. The resulting dataset underwent rigorous preprocessing, including feature engineering, dimensionality reduction via PCA, and class balancing using the Synthetic Minority Over-sampling Technique (SMOTE). A GNN model was developed to capture the complex relational structure inherent in log data and its performance was benchmarked against a suite of traditional machine learning classifiers. The results demonstrate the clear superiority of the graph-based approach, with the GNN achieving an accuracy of 96.56% and an F1-score of 97.93%, significantly outperforming the next-best model, Random Forest (92.04% accuracy). The findings confirm that by modeling the contextual relationships between events, rather than treating them as isolated data points, GNNs provide a more robust and accurate method for identifying malicious activities. Key contributions of this research include the generation of a new, high-fidelity labeled dataset based on realistic attack simulations and empirical evidence that graph-based models can substantially enhance the speed and precision of DFIR processes, offering a powerful tool for modern cybersecurity operations.
