TI  - A Systematic Review of Generative AI Applications in Process Mining: Techniques, Challenges, and Future Directions
AU  - D. Shahin
AU  - E. Yasin
AU  - L. Ghaith
AU  - A. Aref
T2  - 2026 2nd International Conference on Computational Intelligence Approaches and Applications (ICCIAA)
AB  - Process mining uses event logs to understand, monitor, and improve business processes. In practice, many logs are noisy, incomplete, and heterogeneous, which limits conventional pipelines for predictive monitoring, anomaly detection, and process discovery. Generative AI (GenAI), including generative deep models and large language models (LLMs), has recently been explored to support synthetic log generation, learning under scarce supervision, and natural-language interaction with process analytics.This paper reports a systematic literature review following PRISMA guidance. We analyse 24 peer-reviewed studies published from 2020 to January 2025 that apply GenAI and closely related foundation-model approaches to process mining and BPM tasks grounded in event logs. We organise the evidence into five themes: (i) business-oriented assistance and decision support, (ii) predictive process monitoring, (iii) explainability and human-facing interaction, (iv) anomaly detection, and (v) process model generation. Across the reviewed studies, GenAI shows the most consistent value when tasks require generation, interaction, or learning under weak supervision and rare-event constraints. However, the evidence base remains fragmented, with limited standardized benchmarking, uneven reporting of reproducibility details, and recurring concerns around privacy, computational cost, and trust.
