AB  - As modern system logs grow in complexity, deep learning models provide high anomaly detection accuracy but suffer from a lack of interpretability. We propose a modular postprocessing framework that clusters anomalous log sequences to reveal underlying failure modes. Our approach uses a tree-based surrogate model to approximate black-box decision boundaries, utilizing SHAP values to map log sequences into an “explanatory space” of template importance. By clustering within this SHAPspace, we identify latent subgroups of anomalies corresponding to distinct operational issues. Evaluations on HDFS and BGL datasets, as well as proprietary telecommunications data, demonstrate that our pipeline provides granular interpretability and improves detection precision and F1-scores. Results show that identified clusters align with known system stress periods, confirming the method's practical utility for system operators.
