AB  - Microservices architecture has become a preferred solution for building scalable, maintainable, and modular applications by decomposing functionality into independently deployable services. Its synergy with agile methodologies supports rapid development and continuous deployment. Nevertheless, the decentralized and everchanging structure of microservices poses major difficulties in maintaining system stability, especially when it comes to identifying anomalies during execution. Traditional rule-based approaches often lack the flexibility to capture complex contextual behaviors and fail to adapt in agile, fastchanging environments. This study presents an enhanced anomaly detection model for microservices operating in agile settings, utilizing Natural Language Processing (NLP) technique of Word2Vec and a Bidirectional Long Short-Term Memory (BiLSTM) network. System logs are parsed and transformed into semantic embeddings, which are then processed by the BiLSTM to capture temporal and contextual relationships within service interactions. The proposed model effectively identifies both linear and nonlinear anomaly patterns that are often overlooked by conventional techniques. Experimental results demonstrate momentous improvements in detecting accuracy and robustness, validating the model's capability to support proactive monitoring and resilience in microservices-based systems. This approach contributes to strengthening operational stability and observability in agile development environments.
