AU  - P. Chen
AU  - F. Feng
T2  - 2025 Asia Conference on Energy Conversion Systems and Power Electronics (AECSPE)
AB  - Facing the increasingly serious network security threats in power plant operation and maintenance system and the challenges of high-dimensional heterogeneity and complex semantics of alarm logs, this paper proposes a framework for intelligent analysis and attack traceability of high-dimensional alarm logs, which integrates semantic analysis of industrial protocols, construction of dynamic feature space and heterogeneous graph neural network (HGNN). Firstly, the unified representation of multi-source logs is realized by protocol keyword extraction, equipment state coding and time sequence alignment technology; Secondly, the sequential attention mechanism is introduced to fuse network, state and semantic features to construct a dynamic feature space; Furthermore, based on HGNN, the complex association between equipment, log events and alarms is modeled, and anomaly detection and attack path mining are realized. In the aspect of attack traceability, combined with the improved Granger causality test and reinforcement learning (RL) path search mechanism, the causality diagram is constructed and the attack link is dynamically inferred, and the path credibility is verified by power business rules. The experiment is based on real power plant logs and five kinds of new attack simulation data. The results show that the detection rate of this method is 96.3%, the false alarm rate is only 0.42%, the traceability accuracy rate is 94.8%, and the average path search time is 0.87s, which is significantly better than the baseline models such as LSTM-GCN and EAGLE, and verifies its effectiveness and practicability in complex industrial environment.
