AU  - G. Ganesh
AU  - D. Anandaraman
AU  - S. Pavithra
T2  - 2026 4th International Conference on Intelligent Data Communication Technologies and Internet of Things (IDCIoT)
AB  - System log anomaly detection is a critical domain in artificial intelligence, essential for maintaining the security and reliability of distributed computing environments. Conventional log analysis techniques frequently suffer from large amounts of unstructured data, poor interpretability, and sluggish reaction times. Recent works have explored autoencoder-based anomaly detection with SHAP explanations, many rely on KernelSHAP, which is computationally intensive and unsuitable for real-time applications. FastSHAP-XAD (Fast SHapley Additive exPlanations for eXplainable Anomaly Detection, an effective and comprehensible system for unsupervised log anomaly detection is presented in this paper. The approach integrates Isolation Forest for anomaly detection with TreeSHAP to deliver high-speed, local SHAP-based explanations. Anomalies are identified from TF-IDF vectorized log windows, and their root causes are interpreted by highlighting top contributing log tokens. FastSHAP-XAD achieves a maximum 81.1 % detection accuracy with unsupervised learning, an F1-score of 0.055, and enables SHAPbased interpretation at $\mathbf{\sim 1 0 0} \boldsymbol{\times}$ faster latency than KernelSHAP, reducing explanation time from $\sim \mathbf{1 0}$ seconds to approximately 100 milliseconds per log window. An interactive dashboard supports visual analysis, ground- truth comparison, and rootcause diagnosis. This framework bridges the gap between theoretical explainability and real-world deployment in logbased anomaly detection systems.
