AU  - H. K. S
AU  - R. Venkatesan
AU  - K. Ramalakshmi
T2  - 2025 International Conference on Emerging Research in Computational Science (ICERCS)
AB  - The accelerating complexity of enterprise IT environments has led to an upward trajectory in complex, sophisticated cyberattacks that outpace traditional methods of detection. Generally, Security Operation Centres find it difficult to successfully distinguish benign behaviour from malicious activity in real-time, particularly because of the amount of event logs that an organization can collect. This paper presents an intelligent framework for security event log analysis based on Large Language Models (LLMs) with Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG). It extracts insights from raw event log entries into structured insights such as severity classification, malicious activity detection, recommendations for isolation, and personalized incident response plans by integrating curated knowledge bases with LLM reasoning. These security logs are then analysed by state-of-the-art LLMs (Mistral 7B at local deployment with RAG and Google’s Gemini Flash 2.0 API). The proposed approach demonstrates the rationale for LLMs to be an ability to enhance SOC analysts' capabilities with contextual analysis and recommendations for actionable response, directly from event IDs, without any fine-tuning. This study highlights the novelty of applying RAG-driven LLMs to automated security event analysis and positions the framework as a step toward next-generation intelligent SOC operations.
