T2  - 2025 IEEE Smart World Congress (SWC)
AB  - Log anomaly detection is a critical technology for ensuring reliability and operational efficiency in software systems. Traditional methods often face challenges, particularly those related to log parsing complexities that can lead to information loss and consequently high false positive rates. While detection identifies issues, understanding them is also vital. Existing workflow-based approaches offer some explanation by comparing anomalous sequences against normal patterns. There is also a lack of semantic-level anomaly diagnosis capabilities. In this paper, we propose a novel approach named HybirdLog, which focuses on the detection and diagnosis of log anomalies. It introduces heterogeneous semantic collaborative training, integrating anomaly features extracted by LLMs, jointly encoding semantic information and anomaly tendencies. Our approach achieves low-resource cross-domain anomaly detection and interpretability of anomalies. Experimental validation on two public datasets demonstrates the leading performance of our proposed method, offering an efficient and interpretable solution for analyzing logs from software systems.
