TI  - Behavioral Analysis of Sequential Decision Logs using Data Mining Techniques
AU  - K. Chowdary
AU  - D. Jain
AU  - A. Mittal
AU  - G. P. Reddy
AU  - U. Shivani Sri Varshini
T2  - 2026 4th International Conference on Intelligent Cyber Physical Systems and Internet of Things (ICoICI)
AB  - Most current research on IT incident log analysis treats execution records as flat tables for classification or as inputs to process mining pipelines, leaving the sequential behavior contained in state-transition traces largely unexplored. This paper proposes a behavior-aware data mining framework that treats real-world IT incident event logs as ordered execution sequences and analyzes them through a unified pipeline combining behaviorally derived feature engineering, K-Means clustering, Isolation Forest anomaly detection, temporal drift analysis, and supervised Service Level Agreement (SLA) violation prediction. From 119,998 event records spanning 20,769 unique incidents, the framework derives interpretable execution features such as sequence length, unique actions, repetition count, and an instability score. Clustering yields three behavioral classes (efficient, moderate, and unstable), and anomaly detection flags about 5% of traces as outliers. The supervised models discriminate SLA outcomes strongly, with Random Forest reaching an accuracy of 0.82 and an AUC of 0.8797 on the holdout set and 0.8863 under 5-fold stratified cross-validation. Feature importance identifies sys_mod_count as the dominant predictor, while a control LSTM experiment confirms that raw sequence order alone is insufficient, validating the behavioral feature engineering approach.
