AB  - With the rapid expansion of digital networks, traditional signature-based Intrusion Detection Systems (IDS) struggle to detect zero-day attacks due to their dependence on predefined threat patterns. This work proposes a machine learning–based anomaly detection framework integrated with real-time packet monitoring and geospatial visualization to enhance situational awareness. Three models—K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN), Logistic Regression (LR), and Decision Tree Classifier (DTC)—were trained and evaluated on the NSL-KDD dataset following a complete preprocessing and feature reduction pipeline. The DTC model achieved the highest performance with 99% accuracy and balanced precision–recall, outperforming KNN (98%) and LR (93%). The system further incorporates Wireshark-based live traffic capture and KML geospatial mapping to visualize anomaly origins in real time. The results demonstrate that the proposed DTC-based IDS provides high detection accuracy, low latency, and improved interpretability, making it suitable for deployment in large-scale network environments
