TI  - LLM-FPR: Large Language Model-based False Positive Reduction Framework for Network Intrusion Detection
AU  - S. Jiang
T2  - 2026 6th International Symposium on Computer Technology and Information Science (ISCTIS)
AB  - High false positive rates in Network Intrusion Detection Systems remain a significant challenge for Security Operations Centers, often leading to severe alert fatigue. This paper proposes LLM-FPR, a novel four-stage framework that mitigates false alarms by integrating statistical anomaly detection with Large Language Model semantic reasoning. Initially, an ensemble detector module generates preliminary alerts. Subsequently, an LLM module, powered by Retrieval-Augmented Generation, incorporates external domain knowledge to contextually analyze each alert. A final fact-verification layer cross-validates the LLM outputs against established facts to ensure reliability. On benchmark datasets, LLM-FPR achieves an over 80% reduction in false positives without compromising true positive detection. The framework’s capacity to filter spurious alerts and deliver explainable decisions directly mitigates analyst fatigue, significantly improving SOC efficiency.
