TI  - Multiplex Hypergraph Contrastive Learning for Identifying Critical Workflows in Cross-Domain Business Collaboration
AU  - T. -Y. Zuo
AU  - P. Li
AU  - G. Lyu
AU  - Y. Jiang
AU  - K. Di
T2  - IEEE Transactions on Network Science and Engineering
AB  - Modern business collaboration increasingly spans multiple heterogeneous platforms such as enterprise resource planning, manufacturing execution systems, and supply chain management, where identifying critical workflows that coordinate knowledge across platforms is essential for improving collaboration efficiency. Existing methods fail to simultaneously address multi-platform heterogeneity with incompatible knowledge representations, multi-party workflow interactions beyond bilateral relationships, and self-supervised learning from unlabeled industrial data. To address these limitations, we propose a multiplex hypergraph contrastive learning framework that models business collaboration as a multiplex hypergraph where layers represent platform knowledge networks, hyperedges capture multi-party workflows, and inter-layer connections encode cross-domain dependencies. Specifically, we introduce multi-granularity contrastive learning operating at node, hyperedge, and membership levels to align heterogeneous representations, force-guided augmentation that preserves critical structures during perturbation, and representation-guided importance propagation that identifies semantically central workflows in learned embedding space rather than raw topology. Experiments on synthetic datasets, three full-scale BPI benchmarks, and external validation on Sepsis Cases dataset with clinical outcomes, together with structural ablation, parameter sensitivity, ground truth robustness analysis, and expert-oriented validation, show that the proposed framework consistently improves critical workflow ranking quality over 18 baselines spanning centrality methods, hypergraph neural networks, contrastive learning approaches, and process mining techniques.
