AB  - The formally documented standard operating procedure (SOP) serves as the initial step in the management of organizational business processes. SOP models built using the Business Process Model and Notation (BPMN) enable systematic visualization of business processes. Various previous studies have developed graph-based anomaly detection methods; however, most of these approaches still rely on manually constructed process models before being converted into graphs. In many cases, the conversion of BPMN models into a graph-based method is not performed automatically, which may lead to inconsistencies in control flow structures and reduce the accuracy of anomaly detection. This study proposes a graph-based method that automates the conversion of BPMN business process models into a graph method, along with wrong pattern detection focusing on Non-Free Choice and Invisible Task patterns that are often not explicitly detectable in conventional event logs. The proposed method was evaluated on a dataset consisting of 50 process instances (cases), successfully achieving a 1.00 accuracy rate in detecting wrong patterns. This result was manually verified by comparing expected and actual activity sequences, especially in transitions involving invisible tasks and non-free choice patterns. However, this precision should be viewed within the limited scope and scale of the data set.
