TI  - Spiking Neural Networks for Log-Based Anomaly Detection: A Survey, Taxonomy, and Roadmap on Energy-Efficient Neuromorphic Log Analysis
AU  - T. Vu
AU  - D. Do
AU  - T. Bui
AU  - D. -T. Mai
AU  - C. -K. Pham
T2  - IEEE Access
AB  - Log-based anomaly detection is essential for the reliability and security of modern software systems, yet deep learning methods face high energy costs that hinder deployment in resource-constrained settings. Spiking Neural Networks (SNNs), inspired by biological neural mechanisms, offer an energy-efficient alternative through event-driven computation and temporal spike coding. This paper presents the first survey, taxonomy, and roadmap that systematically bridges SNNs with log-based anomaly detection—two fields that have evolved largely in isolation. Because directly established SNN log-anomaly-detection literature is still nascent, we explicitly distinguish between evidence drawn directly from log-anomaly-detection studies and evidence transferred from adjacent SNN, neuromorphic, NLP, and time-series domains; claims based on transferred evidence are clearly marked throughout. We make four contributions. First, we present a comprehensive taxonomy of log-to-spike encoding schemes, SNN architectures (RSNN, CSNN, Spiking Transformer, Spiking Autoencoder), and compatible learning algorithms applicable to log anomaly detection. Second, we survey energy-efficiency evidence from the neuromorphic computing literature and provide a structured mapping between log data characteristics and SNN computational properties, establishing why SNNs are a strong candidate for log analysis. Third, we propose a joint accuracy-efficiency evaluation framework incorporating synaptic operations, energy per inference, and spike sparsity, and we provide illustrative baseline experiments via ANN-to-SNN conversion on HDFS, BGL, and Thunderbird; analytical projections (not hardware measurements) suggest 100–1, $200\times $  energy reduction potential on neuromorphic hardware at a modest 1–3% accuracy trade-off. Fourth, we identify key open challenges and future research directions, including neuromorphic hardware deployment, online continual learning, Spiking GNN architectures for log event graphs, and SNN-based explainability.
