AU  - N. V. Rao
T2  - 2026 2nd International Conference on Innovations in Mechanical, Robotics, Computing and Biomedical Engineering (ICIMRBE)
AB  - As the number of system logs increases with growing microservice architecture complexity, it becomes impossible to monitor all of them manually, leading to the need for automatic systems for detecting system failures. The majority of current anomaly detection techniques focus on the F1 score (a combination of accuracy and sensitivity), which may lead to reduced alerting for early-stage yet severe system failures (False Negatives). In this paper, we proposed an unsupervised anomaly detection framework using a high-sensitivity Transformer Autoencoder ("LogBERT-Lite") to detect system anomalies from unlabeled production logs of OpenStack. Our model uses the self-attention mechanism to learn patterns of normal system behavior in log sequence data. We tested the models' ability to identify 8 out of 11 critical injected system faults as part of our evaluation on 2000 sequences of production logs. While the high-sensitivity configuration of our model leads to a loss in precision (4.37%), the results show that the risk of missing a critical fault is minimized, providing evidence for the effectiveness of the use of a Transformer-based model for a "Recall-First" AIOps strategy, and provides a foundation for future research into reducing the false positive rate without sacrificing failure detection capabilities.
