TI  - Enhancing Intrusion Detection with Transformer Models: A Self-Attention Approach for Log-Based Cyber Threat Analysis
AU  - M. A. H. Tonmoy
AU  - S. M. M. Alam
AU  - M. A. K. Azad
T2  - 2025 IEEE International Conference on Emerging Trends in Computing and Communication (ETCOM)
AB  - System logs are vital for detecting malicious activities but their quantity, complexity, and the continuously changing nature of attack patterns create considerable difficulties for conventional intrusion detection systems. To overcome these challenges, Transformer models utilizing self-attention processes have been studied for their capacity to analyze long-range temporal and semantic connections in diverse log data. This study employed fine-tuned CyberBERT and LogBERT frameworks on publicly accessible log datasets (HDFS, BGL, Thunderbird) and synthetic SIEM-generated traces that replicate real-world attack situations. Performance was evaluated against deep learning baselines (LSTM, GRU, CNN) and traditional anomaly detectors (Isolation Forest, One-Class SVM) employing assessment criteria including Accuracy, Precision, Recall, F1-score, AUC, False Positive/Negative Rates, and detection latency. The proposed SAIDS architecture obtains an F1 score of 95.6 and a PR-AUC of 98.1, exceeding both traditional and Transformer benchmarks. It enhances zero-day detection by up to +12 AUPRC points and decreases miss rates at 1% FPR by approximately 40% compared to LogBERT, while maintaining sub-20 ms latency and throughput above 50k events/s, proving it suitable for real-time SIEM implementation. The feasibility of deployment was confirmed by incorporating the optimal model into an ELK/Splunk pipeline, facilitating real-time inference using lightweight APIs. These findings confirm that self-attention-driven architectures are flexible, reliable, and effective solutions for log-based intrusion detection, with possible applications in multi-source surveillance and continuous learning inside SIEM systems.
