TY  - CONF
TI  - Research on Operating System Log Anomaly Detection Based on Unsupervised Machine Learning
AU  - Y. Wang
T2  - 2026 6th Asia-Pacific Conference on Communications Technology and Computer Science (ACCTCS)
AB  - With the deepening of digitalization, operating system logs have become a core basis for system stability and security monitoring. However, the massive and complex log data presents challenges for traditional detection methods based on rules or keyword matching, such as high annotation costs, lack of semantic information, and insufficient real-time performance. This study aims to construct an efficient unsupervised machine learning log anomaly detection framework to address these key issues. The study first preprocesses the log data by cleaning and standardizing it and extracting multidimensional features (temporal, semantic, and statistical). Subsequently, an unsupervised learning algorithm is used to construct a detection model, and the robustness of the model is improved through multidimensional feature fusion. Experimental results show that the deep autoencoder model performs the best, achieving a detection accuracy of 92.5% and a false alarm rate of only 3.2%, significantly outperforming traditional clustering methods and exhibiting excellent real-time processing capabilities. This study provides an efficient and feasible technical solution for the intelligent operation, maintenance, and security protection of large-scale distributed systems, effectively reducing the reliance on manual annotation for anomaly detection and improving the timeliness and accuracy of system risk warnings.
