TY  - CHAP
TI  - Automatic Detection of Behavioral Changes in a Smart Home
T2  - Artificial Intelligence in Health
AB  - Summary <p>Industrialized countries today are undergoing profound social and economic changes affecting their demographics. Behavioral changes for an elderly person are not necessarily abnormal behaviors. The scientific literature is currently very limited on the subject of long&#x2010;term behavioral changes in the context of smart homes. Elderly people in good general condition tend to behave consistently at home over time. In recent years, extensive studies have been conducted on behavior monitoring in smart homes with applications such as activity recognition, abnormal behavior detection and behavior prediction. In this chapter, the authors propose a new approach for detecting long behavioral changes in a person living alone in a smart home. Future work could evaluate the correlation between the points of change detected by our methodology and life events for the resident, such as health problems or voluntary changes in habits.</p>
