TY  - CONF
TI  - Advanced Artificial Intelligence Techniques for Automated Identification of Anomalous Events in Log Data
AU  - P. K. Adapala
AU  - B. A
T2  - 2026 IEEE Global Symposium on Emerging and Communication Technologies (GSEACT)
AB  - Analyzing log data produced by systems being distributed is of primary importance for determining irregularities in system operation behavior and pinpointing security risks. The more log data there is the more it scales in complexity which makes it unlikely to be feasible to monitor log data scanners in their current form. This paper analyzes various artificial intelligence mechanisms for log data abnormality and empirically demonstrates an optimized method for identification to increase computing utility in detecting anomalies. The design conjecture within this paper for an anomalous log event detection system uses semantic log parsing and deep neural sequential modeling for embedding logs with context and determining the temporality of event clusters. The proposed system model in this paper was evaluated on the preestablished standard log datasets of HDFS and BGL which consisted of more than one million log entries. Empirically, the system model was able to surpass the detection, accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-score, of all log event detection and analysis systems of DeepLog (91.2%), LogAnomaly (92.6%), LogBERT (95.1%), Transformers-AE (95.7%), with an accuracy of 97.3%, precision of 96.6%, recall of 96.9%, and an F1-score of 96.7%. Moreover, the developed framework increased the computing utility with the detection of anomalous log pattern frameworks by an increase of 98 ms for detection latency, and 640 MB for memory utilization. The proposed framework is able to identify anomalous log patterns within a data set with an AUC score of 0.99 which demonstrates strong efficacy in distinguishing anomalous logs from normal logs. The proposed framework is empirically demonstrated to provide improved reliability and mobility for large volumes of log monitoring to identify anomalous logs. The results show the value of cutting-edge AI techniques in the automated detection of log anomalies and the role these techniques play in contemporary cybersecurity and system dependability.
