AB  - The increasing volume and velocity of network traffic and the rising sophistication of cyber threats have made real-time intrusion detection a critical necessity for modern infrastructures. Traditional security systems often rely on static rules or batch analysis, limiting their ability to detect novel or rapidly evolving attacks. This paper proposes a hybrid architecture that integrates stream processing frameworks with deep learning models for real-time threat detection on network logs. The system utilizes Apache Kafka for log ingestion, Apache Flink for stream analytics, deep neural networks, specifically Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM), and one-dimensional Convolutional Neural Networks (1D CNN) for anomaly detection. We evaluate our solution using benchmark datasets, including CIC-IDS 2017 and UNSW-NB15. The experimental results demonstrate that our system can detect threats with high accuracy and low latency, maintaining scalability under high-throughput conditions. The proposed architecture is suitable for deployment in operational environments where timely and accurate threat detection is essential.
