T2  - 2026 22nd IEEE International Colloquium on Signal Processing & Its Applications (CSPA)
AB  - In modern computing systems, identifying abnormal system behavior can be more challenging than solving the issue. System logs can be used to detect abnormal behavior since they contain detailed information about all system events. To explore an effective approach for this task, this study compares detection accuracy and inference speed between LSTM and Transformer models for supervised log anomaly detection. Our experimental results with a window size of 20 show that on the BGL dataset, the Transformer achieves a higher F1-score of 0.975, while the LSTM achieves 0.836. On the Thunderbird dataset, LSTM performs slightly better with an F1-score of 0.997 compared to 0.982 for the Transformer. The results also show that a short context window size of 20 provides better detection accuracy and inference speed performance for both models, while a long context window size of $\mathbf{1 0 0}$ does not provide better results. In addition, the Transformer processes log sequences faster and achieves higher throughput than the LSTM due to its parallel computation capability.
