TY  - CONF
TI  - Distributed System Log Anomaly Detection Using Bidirectional LSTM with Attention Mechanism
AU  - Y. Ouyang
AU  - Z. Ye
AU  - X. Cao
AU  - Y. Chang
T2  - 2026 3rd International Conference on Smart Grid and Artificial Intelligence (SGAI)
AB  - With the rapid deployment of smart grid infrastructure and the proliferation of distributed energy management systems, the operational reliability and cybersecurity of power grid IT platforms have become increasingly critical concerns. Log anomaly detection serves as a fundamental technique for ensuring the stable operation of these complex distributed systems. However, existing deep learning approaches face significant challenges: unidirectional models (e.g., standard LSTMs) struggle to capture the complex, global contextual dependencies of concurrently interleaved logs, while large-scale architectures (e.g., Transformers) incur prohibitive computational overhead, making them unsuitable for deployment on resourceconstrained edge devices widely adopted in smart grid field terminals. To address these limitations, this paper proposes SGAI, a novel and lightweight log anomaly detection method integrating a Bidirectional Long Short-Term Memory (Bi-LSTM) network with a token-level Self-Attention mechanism, specifically designed for distributed monitoring systems in smart grid environments. The Bi-LSTM layer extracts both forward and backward temporal dependencies from parsed log sequences, effectively modeling the global context of concurrent events. Subsequently, the attention layer dynamically assigns higher weights to critical fault signals, filtering out redundant operational noise. Extensive experiments conducted on a mixed dataset comprising the public HDFS benchmark and real-world power grid cluster logs demonstrate the superiority of our approach. The proposed SGAI model achieves an F1-score of 0.94, outperforming the classic DeepLog model by 8% and achieving comparable accuracy to standard Transformers. Furthermore, deployment evaluations reveal that our model requires only 14.0 MB of memory and 18 ms of inference latency per sequence, reducing computational overhead by over 60% compared to Transformer architectures. These results prove that SGAI achieves an optimal balance between high detection accuracy and lightweight deployment, making it highly practical for real-time anomaly detection on edge computing nodes deployed across smart grid substations and field operation terminals.
