TI  - STLAD: Sparse-Attention and Temporal- Decomposition for Scalable Log Anomaly Detection
AU  - Y. Xie
AU  - Y. Wang
AU  - K. Feng
AU  - L. Chen
AU  - J. Wang
T2  - IEEE Access
AB  - Log anomaly detection is essential for maintaining the reliability and security of computing systems. Existing methods are hindered by high computational complexity and insufficient modeling of temporal patterns, which limits their scalability and sensitivity. To address these limitations, we present STLAD (Log Anomaly Detection with Sparse Attention and Temporal Feature), a framework that jointly models global dependencies and localized temporal patterns in log sequences. STLAD employs two complementary pathways to balance accuracy and efficiency. The global attention branch leverages Squeeze-and-Excitation attention and probSparse self-attention, which selectively focuses on the most informative queries to capture long-range dependencies while reducing the quadratic cost of standard attention. In parallel, the local feature branch performs temporal decomposition to separate log sequences into trend and seasonal components, and then applies CNNs to capture short-term variations. These two branches are combined via a gated fusion mechanism that adaptively balances their contributions, enabling emphasis on global context or local details for robust detection. Experimental results demonstrate that STLAD achieves absolute improvements in F1-score ranging from 1.46% to 5.66% across four benchmark datasets (HDFS, BGL, Thunderbird, and Zookeeper), outperforming nine state-of-the-art methods, including DeepLog, LogBERT, and ColaLLM. These results demonstrate that our method improves detection accuracy on benchmarks and enhances scalability and timeliness for rapid failure or attack detection in real-world systems.
