AB  - Monitoring systems, devices, and events throughout an organization can be a monumental task. This chapter explores how security monitoring data is captured and analyzed. It provides the details of how endpoint and network monitoring techniques and technologies are applied as well as some of the artifacts that security analysts commonly look for. The chapter then explores logging tools, how to access common logs on Windows and Linux systems, and the log review concepts that are used to analyze firewall, Intrusion Detection System/Intrusion Prevention System, system, and event logs. The foundation of most security designs relies on the concept of <i>defense&#x2010;in&#x2010;depth</i>. Security analysts need to know what logs exist by default on systems, how to access them, how to find information about the content of those logs, and how to interpret that content. Although there are many types of firewall logs, most have some similarities.
