TI  - Log-Augmented Time-LLM for Resources Prediction in Microservices Architecture
AU  - Z. Lu
AU  - X. Bian
AU  - K. Yu
AU  - X. Wu
T2  - 2025 9th IEEE International Conference on Network Intelligence and Digital Content (IC-NIDC)
AB  - Microservice Architecture is a cloud native architectural pattern that decomposes monolithic applications into loosely coupled, independently deployable lightweight services. With the evolution of Internet technologies, this architecture has gained widespread adoption in distributed systems. The dynamic nature of microservice architectures makes resource prediction paramount for ensuring service quality and cost efficiency. Nevertheless, conventional temporal models fundamentally suffer from semantic context deficiency. In the frontier of multimodal fusion research, the Time-Series Forecasting by Reprogramming Large Language Models(Time-LLM) leveraged prompt engineering to reprogram language models (e.g., Llama) for zero-shot time-series forecasting. However, these approaches extrapolate solely from numerical metrics while ignoring event-driven dynamics encoded in system logs, resulting in substantial prediction errors during burst load scenarios. To transcend this limitation, in this paper, we first design a simulated microservice system based on Docker and Java Virtual Machine. Secondly, we construct a temporal-log-synchronized dataset within microservice architecture, through precise alignment of highresolution performance metrics and event logs. Third, we design the Log-Augmented Time-LLM (LAT-LLM) frame-work, which employs a log-retrieval module to convert event descriptors into natural language prompts, dynamically steering the predictive attention mechanism. Experimental results demonstrate that LAT-LLM significantly enhances prediction accuracy compared to baseline models without log input.
