T2  - 2026 9th International Conference on Intelligent Computing and Control Systems (ICICCS)
AB  - Data exfiltration detection is an essential task in ensuring data security and privacy in cloud environments, as traditional rule-based and signature-driven methods fail to detect sophisticated or novel exfiltration techniques due to their reliance on pre-defined patterns. In this work, we propose a novel approach to detect data exfiltration by analyzing cloud log data using Bidirectional Transformers, such as BERT, which provide a deep semantic modeling of log entries using the bidirectional self-attention mechanism of Transformer architectures to leverage the contextual information of past and future tokens for robust anomaly detection. The method preprocesses and tokenizes raw cloud logs, and embeds them as sequences using a pre-trained or fine-tuned Bidirectional Transformer model, which can capture context to effectively identify suspicious log entries without strict parsing or handengineered features. Anomaly detection is achieved through Masked Language Modeling and unsupervised outlier identification within the embedding space, which experimental results on system log datasets show to have higher accuracy in detecting anomalous exfiltration patterns than traditional methods. The model can generalize across heterogeneous cloud logs, is robust to changing attack strategies, and is robust to data imbalance and noisy records.
