AU  - S. G. R. J
AU  - P. K. S
AU  - J. Umamageswaran
T2  - 2025 IEEE International Conference on Innovate for Humanitarian: Tech Solutions for Global Challenge (ICIH)
AB  - The cybersecurity scheme arises with autonomous vehicles AVs that rely on connected components–the sensor, control system and V2X communications–that may come under attack by GPS spoofing, CAN bus injection, and sensor tampering. Such traditional defenses do not provide adaptability and scalable testing environments. The paper presents SecureSimAV a software approach for ethical hacking based on the Carla simulator to test, detect, and mitigate AV cyberattacks in a controlled environment. SecureSimAV consists of i attack injection modules, ii machine learning-based intrusion detection agents, and iii a threat log and response system. This includes a survey of AV vulnerabilities through perception, control, and communication layers, along with some functional pseudocode that reveals how these integrated systems operate. The SecureSimAV platform offers low cost, reproducibility, scalability, and a secure environment for researchers and developers to probe the security of an AV without risking assets in the real world. It fosters an environment that supports the building of secure and resilient autonomous vehicle ecosystems through responsible and proactive cybersecurity testing.
