AB  - This chapter focuses on server security. It explains the role of network administrators and the importance of server software security. The chapter explores the two classes of users in a network and network authentication options. It also focuses on how to establish resource controls and to maintain server security. The chapter describes some of the various implementations found in different types of networking environments: general&#x2010;purpose servers, appliance servers, application servers, mail servers, firewall servers, proxy servers, web servers, database servers, terminal servers, router servers, bridge servers and so on. Servers require special security consideration and placement within the network. Network administrators use another implementation to keep information secure&#x2014;authentication, authorization, and accounting (AAA). At a most fundamental level, network administrators must have control over their physical server environment to provide a comprehensive security setting. Role&#x2010;based access control (RBAC) is a strategy designed for centralized control of all network objects and users.
