TI  - MGALM: Interpretability-Enhanced APT Detection Using Masked Graph Autoencoders and Large Language Models
AU  - Y. Du
AU  - X. Zhang
AU  - Y. Li
AU  - J. Feng
AU  - Y. Wen
T2  - 2025 IEEE International Symposium on Parallel and Distributed Processing with Applications (ISPA)
AB  - The evolving landscape of cybersecurity threats highlights growing concern over Advanced Persistent Threats (APTs), which pose significant risks to governments and organizations due to their stealthy, multi-stage, and sophisticated tactics. Traditional Intrusion Detection Systems (IDSes) have struggled to detect APTs effectively, leading to the development of provenance-based IDSes that utilize system logs to construct provenance graphs for threat detection. However, provenancebased IDSes still face several challenges, including the need for extensive manual effort, the inability to detect unseen attack patterns, difficulties in balancing detection granularity, efficiency, and interpretability, and insufficient modeling of temporal dynamics. In this paper, we introduce MGALM, a novel self-supervised anomaly detection method specifically designed for APT detection without requiring labeled attack event data. MGALM converts system logs into a stream of provenance subgraphs, uses a masked graph autoencoder to extract high-quality graph vectors, and leverages Transformers to capture temporal dynamics between these vectors for anomaly detection. For detected anomalous subgraphs, MGALM identifies the edges most likely to cause the attack, providing event-level, contextual, and interpretable detection results. Additionally, MGALM incorporates Large Language Models (LLMs) to enhance the interpretability of detection results and reduce the burden on security experts. By focusing on abnormal subgraphs, MGALM supports real-time streaming detection, reducing computational and memory overhead. Our evaluation on two widely used datasets demonstrates its effectiveness, efficiency, and robustness against adversarial attacks.
