TY  - CONF
TI  - Multi-Source Data Fusion and Anomaly Detection Algorithm Design for Smart Grids
AU  - W. Di
AU  - Z. Fan
T2  - 2025 7th International Academic Exchange Conference on Science and Technology Innovation (IAECST)
AB  - Smart grid platforms collect heterogeneous data from phasor measurement units, intelligent electronic devices, distributed sensors, and load monitoring systems. These data streams differ in sampling rate, quality, and semantics, and they are often contaminated by noise or partial failures. Conventional anomaly detection methods usually handle each source independently or fuse them crudely, which degrades performance when the grid enters complex operating states. To address these issues, this paper proposes a multi-source data fusion and anomaly detection algorithm based on improved weighted evidence theory and a deep neural network that combines convolutional neural networks (CNNs) with an attention-enhanced long short-term memory (LSTM) module. First, data from 12 monitoring nodes are normalized, aligned in time, and evaluated by a set of reliability indicators. An improved weighted Dempster–Shafer evidence theory is then used to fuse node-level evidence, where the weights adapt to conflict levels and node reliability so that inconsistent sources are down-weighted and consistent information is strengthened. The fused multi-source representation is then fed to a hybrid CNN–attention–LSTM model. The CNN extracts local spatio-temporal patterns, while a channel-wise attention mechanism highlights salient anomaly-related features before they are passed to the LSTM to model long-term temporal dependencies. Experiments on a multi-source smart grid dataset with simulated faults, topology changes, cyber– physical attacks, and load anomalies show that the proposed method improves F1-score by 3.8–7.6 percentage points and AUC by 2.1–4.5 percentage points over strong baselines. Ablation studies confirm that both the improved weighted evidence fusion and the attention mechanism contribute significantly to performance. The results indicate that the proposed framework can provide robust, accurate anomaly detection under heterogeneous smart grid data, supporting safe and stable grid operation.
