TY  - CONF
TI  - The Role of Time-Sensitive Networking in Enhancing Real-Time Clock Synchronization for Power Monitoring Devices
AU  - J. Hei
AU  - X. Chen
AU  - B. Liu
AU  - Y. Wang
AU  - L. Zhang
AU  - Q. Yao
T2  - 2026 6th International Conference on Recent Trends in Computer Science and Technology (ICRTCST)
AB  - Real-time clock synchronisation to achieve the accurate measurement, data stability and the system stability is important and required in smart grids and industrial automation. In this study, Time Sensitive Networking (TSN) is explored to enhance accuracy and stability of real-time clock-synchronisation by minimising synchronisation errors. The quantitative analysis is done using Python (Google Colab) in order to simulate network conditions (latency, jitter, and packet loss). Machine learning models with advanced machine learning, XGBoost, LightGBM and LSTM are used to forecast the synchronization accuracy and benchmarking is conducted to IEEE 1588 Precision Time Protocol (PTP) in the absence of TSN. The findings show that a much smaller median error (0.5 ms) is obtained with TSN-enabled synchronization than with non-TSN (1.5 ms) as shown in Figure 1. The statistical analyses prove a very significant decrease in synchronization errors ($\mathbf{p}< 0.0001$) proving that TSN is more effective in reducing timing differences introduced by networks. LSTM is more successful than XGBoost and LightGBM to predict synchronization errors (Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) of LSTM is 0.7033 compared to XGBoost (0.7335) and LightGBM (0.7243)). Through real time power monitoring systems, accuracy and reliability in synchronisation can be achieved which is very good as compared to other solutions such as estimation of frequency and generation. Thus, these systems can be served by TSN. The power utilities and the industrial networks should be made to operate using synchronisation based on TSN, and machine learning can be used to optimize dynamically. It leads to practical implications in order to clean-up the event logging, to detect faults better, and to improve the accuracy of the energy distribution. Although the viability is demonstrated in the case of simulation, the capability of the given study is constrained by simulation-based method and a real TSN hardware implementation study would need to be further confirmed.
