TI  - Dual-Ensembling for Robust Log Anomaly Detection: Combining Multi-Scale Inputs and Test-Time Augmentation
AU  - B. Ji
AU  - F. Ji
AU  - X. Li
AU  - J. Ruan
T2  - 2025 5th International Conference on Electronic Information Engineering and Computer Science (EIECS)
AB  - Self-supervised learning with stochastic masking is a prominent technique for log anomaly detection. However, existing approaches suffer from two key limitations: 1) the use of a fixed sliding window restricts their ability to capture anomalous patterns across multiple timescales, and 2) reliance on a single random mask during inference leads to prediction instability. This paper introduces a framework designed to overcome these limitations through enhancements in both data representation and inference strategy. Specifically, we employ a multi-scale sampling strategy to build robust contextual representations and a test-time augmentation mechanism to ensure reliable predictions. Comprehensive evaluations on benchmark datasets, including HDFS, BGL, Thunderbird, and OpenStack, demonstrate the effectiveness of our proposed method, which achieves a state-of-the-art average $\mathbf{F 1}$-score of $\mathbf{9 6. 3 1 \%}$.
