AB  - The rapid advancement of information technology has led to the widespread deployment of software-intensive systems across critical societal infrastructures and core business domains. Ensuring system security and reliability has thus become an imperative challenge. As vital records of system operational states, logs play a central role in automated analysis for system maintenance. To address this, we propose MVLAD (Multi-View learning-based Log Anomaly Detection), a method for log anomaly detection leveraging multi-view information. MVLAD first extracts raw log information from distinct perspectives using a semantic parsing model and a domain-specific tokenization algorithm. Subsequently, it employs a BERT-based feature extraction model to derive view-specific features, which are then processed by SVM classifier to compute anomaly probabilities for each view. Finally, an ensemble learning strategy integrates these probabilities to determine anomaly existence. To enhance practical utility, DeepSeek-R1 is utilized to explain anomalies and provide remediation recommendations. Evaluations on the BGL and Thunderbird datasets demonstrate superior performance, achieving F1-scores of 0.9705 and 0.9986, respectively.
