TI  - Intrusion Detections
AU  - Jie Wang
AU  - Zachary A. Kissel
T2  - Introduction to Network Security: Theory and Practice
AB  - This chapter introduces common intrusion detection techniques and honeypot techniques. Intrusion detection may be carried out at the network level, or at the host level, or both. The first type of intrusion detection is referred to as network&#x2010;based detection (NBD), the second type as host&#x2010;based detection, and the third type as hybrid detection (HBD). An NBD is responsible for checking packets in the network, identifying which packets are yellow&#x2010;light behaviors and which are red&#x2010;light behaviors, and sending warning messages to the alarm manager in the command console. Signature detections and statistical analysis are major intrusion detection techniques used in both NBDs and HBDs. Typical intrusion detection systems (IDS) use one of the following three methods for modeling signatures: built&#x2010;in system, programming system, and expert system. The chapter also discusses behavioral data forensics, which studies how to use data mining techniques to analyze event logs and search for useful information.
