TY  - CONF
TI  - AI-Augmented Automated Triage for System Error Reporting Using Log and Memory Dump Analysis
AU  - Y. Li
T2  - 2026 7th International Conference on Big Data & Artificial Intelligence & Software Engineering (ICBASE)
AB  - Automated error triage is critical for maintaining large-scale computing systems, yet most approaches rely exclusively on system logs as the sole data modality. This paper presents an empirical study demonstrating the fundamental limitations of log-only triage through three controlled experiments on the Blue Gene/L (BGL) supercomputer dataset (4.7M log lines, 41 alert types, 7 months of operation). We find that: (1) removing log message text reduces triage F1 from 0.999 to 0.664 (a 33.5% drop); (2) completely novel error types that have never been seen in training data achieve 0% detection rate; and (3) when trained on early data and tested on later data, recall degrades from 99.8% to 54.4% due to distribution shift in alert types. These findings reveal three intrinsic gaps—text dependence, novelty blindness, and temporal brittleness—that cannot be closed by improving log-based models alone. We argue that multi-modal approaches combining logs, memory dumps, and system metrics are necessary for robust, production-grade automated triage.
