AB  - Estimating the density log is essential in analyzing reservoir characterization of oil and gas exploration. Density and Sonic logs assist in generating the acoustic impedance logs that further help in the impedance volume generation and inversion analysis. In the past different empirical methods (such as Gardner's equation, and Faust Equation) have been proposed to estimate density logs from the other recorded well log data like Sonic and Resistivity Logs. The current study demonstrates that if the Gamma Ray (GR) log is present in a well in addition to the Sonic log, it may be applied to estimate the density logs, improving the acceptance and accuracy of the prediction. The approach takes data from the F3 North field of the Netherlands and estimates density using both gamma and sonic logs. Data from four wells have been used in the study. The model is prepared using training data of three wells and one well is used for testing purposes. The result shows significant improvement by adding GR logs instead of using only Sonic logs while predicting Density with the Neural Network Algorithm. The study gives confidence in using GR logs in addition to Sonic logs for better predictability of Density with Neural Network Algorithms.
