T2  - 2026 International Conference on Emerging Research in Smart Electronics and Machine Informatics (ECMI)
AB  - The exponential growth of web applications and cloud platforms has generated vast log data essential for detecting system failures and cyberattacks. Traditional rule-based intrusion detection systems struggle with high-dimensional, unstructured logs, causing false positives and delayed responses. This paper proposes a machine learning-based anomaly detection framework combining Isolation Forest, Autoencoders, and LSTM models to identify threats such as SQL injection, brute force, DDoS, and bot traffic. The system preprocesses raw logs, extracts features like request frequency, error rates, suspicious keywords, and bot activity, then applies detection models to uncover anomalies. A Streamlit dashboard offers visualization, real-time alerts, and automatic IP blocking. Synthetic datasets generated via custom log simulators test the system's robustness. Experimental results show the framework achieves high accuracy, scalability, and interpretability. This approach bridges traditional static monitoring and adaptive learning techniques, providing a practical, deployable solution for securing enterprises, cloud infrastructures, and IoT ecosystems.
