TI  - Event Camera and DVL-Aided Multi-Sensor Underwater SLAM Under Illumination Variations and Visual Degradation
AU  - Z. Wang
AU  - T. Gao
AU  - W. Midgley
T2  - OCEANS 2026 Sanya
AB  - Reliable localization remains a fundamental challenge for underwater vehicles operating in visually degraded environments characterized by turbidity, low illumination, and light scattering. Vision-based Simultaneous Localization And Mapping (SLAM) systems offer high spatial resolution but degrade severely under poor visibility, whereas acoustic sensing alone suffers from limited resolution and near-field blind zones. This paper presents a tightly coupled multi-sensor underwater SLAM framework that integrates stereo vision, inertial sensing, multi-beam sonar, barometer, Doppler Velocity Log (DVL), and an event camera within a unified sliding-window factor-graph formulation. The proposed system extends conventional sonarvisual-inertial SLAM by incorporating event-camera-derived time surface observations and DVL-derived velocity constraints, with barometer depth measurements used as keyframe anchors, and loop-closure constraints improving global consistency. The system is implemented on a BlueROV2 platform with full ROS 2 integration and evaluated in a turbid harbor environment. Experimental results from a 154.6 m harbor trajectory show a reduction in absolute trajectory root-mean-square error from 3.40 m to 1.45 m in translation and from $\mathbf{2 3. 1 1}^{\circ}$ to $\mathbf{1 3. 2 5}^{\circ}$ in rotation compared to the SVIn2 baseline, while maintaining stable tracking under severe visual degradation.
