TI  - Secure and Privacy-Preserving Machine Learning for Cloud Intrusion Detection with Federated Learning and Generated Attack Data
AU  - P. Ghadekar
AU  - N. Gulhane
AU  - H. Patil
AU  - I. Ramdasi
AU  - G. Hote
AU  - S. Ghodke
T2  - 2025 International Conference on Data, Energy and Communication Networks (DECoN)
AB  - The growing popularity of cloud services, which provides a large and appealing area for attacks, places immense pressure on the defense mechanisms to protect the privacy of the user. We present an inclusive security architecture that incorporates multiple cutting-edge solutions to traverse this challenging landscape. We train an Isolation Forest anomaly detector across the network of disperse clients by using Federated Learning, an approach that fundamentally inhibits the centralization of confidential data. This collaborative operation is secured by an effective privacy architecture: the CKKS strategy permits computation over encrypted data, whereas the Secure Multi-Party Computation ensures the secure and discreet combination of the model updates. This technique overcomes the typical problem of scarce attack data by employing the use of Generative Adversarial Networks for the construction of naturalistic, artificial attack vectors, enhancing the forecast quality of the model. The Ethereum blockchain, which forms the basis of this architecture, records all significant operations, including model modifications and security alerts, creating an auditable and immutable record. The framework demonstrates its value by producing high detection rates and an exceptional F1-Score when tested against practical benchmarks. Finally, our results show that privacy and performance may be successfully combined, providing a workable model for safe cloud operations.
