TI  - IoTector: An Integrated Platform for Monitoring and Intelligent Attack Detection in IoT Networks
AU  - Y. Tursynbek
AU  - N. Albanbay
AU  - D. Djenouri
AU  - S. Latif
AU  - I. Samat
AU  - A. Akhankyzy
T2  - 2026 IEEE 6th International Conference on Smart Information Systems and Technologies (SIST)
AB  - Existing intrusion detection system (IDS) research for Internet of Things (IoT) networks has focused on centralized learning and attack-detection architectures, leading to the development of predominantly centralized IDS platforms. While such approaches simplify system coordination, they may not always be suitable for decentralized IoT environments, where traffic data are distributed across autonomous devices and reduce communication network traffic. In this paper, we present IoTector, a monitoring-oriented intrusion detection platform designed for decentralized IoT environments. IoTector enables multiple distributed IoT clients to collaboratively train deep learning–based intrusion detection models using local traffic data under a FedAvg-based federated learning paradigm, without sharing raw data. The platform is evaluated across configurations of up to 10 distributed clients over 20 communication rounds. The platform supports continuous traffic capture, event logging and alerting, device-level monitoring, and interactive visualization within a unified web-based environment. To evaluate the proposed platform, multiple deep learning architectures, including DNN, CNN, and CNN–LSTM, are evaluated on the CICIoT2023 dataset (80/20 train-test split) within the IoTector framework. Experimental results from pilot deployment show that the CNN-based model delivers the best performance, achieving 95.27% accuracy, a 94.92% F1-score, and an average inference latency of approximately 1.4 ms per sample, which validates IoTector as a practical system-level platform that bridges the gap between IDS research and operational deployment in decentralized IoT environments.
