TY  - CONF
TI  - VectorSight: A Domain-Adaptive Framework for Multi-Modal Observability Signal Classification
AU  - R. Goturkarnam
AU  - S. Kumar
AU  - H. Palitanawala
T2  - SoutheastCon 2026
AB  - Modern observability systems generate massive volumes of unstructured logs challenging traditional monitoring approaches. This paper presents VectorSight, a domain-adaptive framework for log classification using transformer-based embeddings and vector databases. VectorSight combines Drain3 template mining with fine-tuned DistilBERT for log relevance classification (Relevant, Irrelevant, Unknown), storing embeddings in Qdrant for efficient semantic search and temporal correlation analysis. We demonstrate transfer learning capabilities across supercomputer log domains through Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA), enabling rapid deployment to new environments with minimal retraining overhead. Evaluated on BGL (Blue Gene/L) and Thunderbird benchmarks using binary Alert/Normal labels, VectorSight achieves 99.95 % accuracy and 97.44 % F1-score on BGL baseline ($\mathbf{1 7 4 K}$ samples) and $\mathbf{9 9. 7 9 \%}$ F1-score with $\mathbf{1 0 0 \%}$ recall on Thunderbird (500 K test samples, 98.5 % class imbalance) through domain adaptation. The framework implements semantic search using 768-dimensional embeddings and heuristic temporal correlation scoring to surface related events for root cause investigation. Reproducibility analysis across five random seeds shows consistent performance with low variance $(\pm 0.3 \%)$. Metrics are processed into semantic embeddings and stored in a vector database to enable cross-modal correlation with logs; advanced forecasting-based analysis is outside the scope of this study.
