AU  - H. S. Negi
AU  - D. Negi
AU  - A. Khan
AU  - J. Harbola
T2  - 2026 International Conference on Computational Intelligence Systems and Devices (ICCISD)
AB  - The cyber threats have grown exponentially generating novel difficulties in ensuring security in distributed and privacy-sensitive settings. The conventional centralized intrusion detection systems (IDS) tend to demand pooling of sensitive data in networks, a fact that constitutes a serious privacy dilemma. In this paper, the author will suggest Federated Adversarial Defense Network (FADN) to provide privacy-preserving cyber threat detection using multi-source log data. The suggested framework combines federated learning (FL) with adversarial robust neural architecture to work together to learn attack signatures using decentralized data without data sharing. The model was also trained and tested on a public network of cyber security attacks data consisting of more than 40,000 network flows records that included protocol metadata, anomaly scores, and IDS/IPS alerts. Experimental findings prove that FADN can provide high precision (97.6%) and reduce adversarial attacks and secure data privacy among distributed clients. The comparative analysis on centralized baselines demonstrates that the level of privacy leakage is highly reduced and the resistance to gradient-based adversarial perturbations is enhanced. The study adds a scalable and explained federated defense framework on real-time cyber threat intelligence on heterogeneous environments.
