TI  - Sector-Scanning Ultrasonic Radar-Emulation System for Short-Range UAV Intrusion Detection
AU  - V. U
AU  - N. M
AU  - N. G
AU  - N. M
T2  - 2026 6th International Conference on Trends in Material Science and Inventive Materials (ICTMIM)
AB  - Low altitude UAV intrusion is an emerging security threat in remote border areas, whereby constant human manpower and aerial surveillance are costly in terms of operations and are influenced by weather conditions. In this paper, a low-budget radar-emulation prototype sector-scanning surveillance structure based on an ESP32 microcontroller and an ultrasonic time-of-flight (TOF) sensor is described. The system conducts an indexed 360° environmental scanning in eight angular sectors with the help of a stepper-motor-driven sensing assembly. Local processing of distance measurements is done by use of temporal filtering to remove spurious reflections and minimize false alarms. Authenticated cases of intrusion are sent over Wi-Fi to a remote monitoring station, where a visualization program written in MATLAB is used to give a real-time polar plot and a log of events. Under conditions of lowaltitude intrusion experiments, it has been experimentally validated that the directional detection has high reliability at a radius of 4 m, an average alert latency of 0.82 s, and 90% detection consistency in repeated tests. The intended architecture is a scalable prototype platform of radar-assisted border surveillance studies, focusing on modularity, powerefficiency, and cost-efficient implementation on resourceconstrained deployments.
