AU  - P. M
AU  - R. Singh
AU  - J. Gupta
AU  - T. Banka
T2  - 2026 18th International Conference on COMmunication Systems and NETworks (COMSNETS)
AB  - Modern enterprise infrastructures increasingly rely on microservices, hybrid multicloud platforms, and distributed networks, making cross-layer root cause analysis (RCA) a significant challenge. System failures often span the application, platform, and network layers, yet conventional metric-based RCA approaches are limited in their ability to associate anomalous metrics with the high volume of unstructured and heterogeneous log data. Although logs contain valuable diagnostic context, their scale, diversity, and temporal misalignment hinder effective correlation, leading to prolonged mean time to resolution (MTTR). This paper presents a real-time, topology-aware framework for log–metric correlation in hybrid cloud and distributed network environments. The proposed framework employs multi-dimensional scoring to annotate log events and integrates a correlation engine that aligns these events with anomalous performance metrics using topology constraints and temporal synchronization. A Large Language Model–driven module, referred to as RCA Copilot, leverages these correlated signals to generate succinct, human-readable diagnostic summaries and to interpret cross-layer anomalies. By facilitating critical log retrieval, cross-layer dependency awareness, and structured, template-based statistical analysis, the framework provides actionable insights that accelerate fault diagnosis and resolution. Through efficient temporal alignment and integration of heterogeneous log sources, it enhances diagnostic precision, reduces MTTR, and supports autonomous, explainable observability across hybrid multicloud environments.
