AU  - M. Pendyala
T2  - 2026 4th International Conference on Intelligent Cyber Physical Systems and Internet of Things (ICoICI)
AB  - The Modern cloud infrastructures utilize microservices that offer scalability, flexibility, and resilience. However, traditional models fail to capture the intricate logical dependencies and causal links between distributed components. In addition, fragmented log data across the abstraction layers make manual correlation impossible, leading to critical visibility gaps. This study proposes a hybrid framework that combines a Dynamic Knowledge Graph (DKG) and Temporal Logical Attention Network (TLAN). The DKG helps reconstruct physical-to-virtual structural relationships using an adaptive adjacency matrix that includes Istio traces, raw logs, and Kubernetes orchestration metadata. The TLAN incorporates parallel multi-scale feature extraction, followed by the fusion of bidirectional long short-term memory (BiLSTM) and Graph Attention Network (GAT) to capture sequential patterns and topological dependencies simultaneously. The DKG-TLAN model utilizes cross-layer interdependencies between logical services and physical infrastructure. The temporal and structural patterns are fused by a learnable gating mechanism to identify the common physical point of failure in microservices. The experimental results in the Sock Shop environment demonstrate that the proposed method effectively detects anomalies and facilitates automated services to maintain system resilience.
