AB  - The increasing complexity of university admission processes has intensified the need for integrated digital platforms that support efficiency, transparency, and responsive interaction. However, institutions often lack visibility into how applicants engage with these systems. This study investigates user interaction behavior by analyzing communication log data generated from the integration of Oracle PeopleSoft Campus Solutions (OPCS) and Salesforce CRM within the admission system at Universitas Pelita Harapan (UPH), Jakarta, Indonesia. Using descriptive analysis with Microsoft Excel Pivot Tables, the study examines the total volume of logs and their distribution across academic programs. Results show significantly higher engagement in Master of Notary (271 logs), Master of Law (177), and Master of Management (165), while Nursing and Master of Tourism exhibit minimal activity. System-generated log types such as READ, REEB, RERL, and REWE appear consistently across programs, reflecting stable system operations. The findings demonstrate the value of log analysis for improving system performance and supporting data driven academic decision-making.
