TI  - Towards Robust SSH Anomaly Detection: A Deep Learning Comparative Analysis
AU  - M. W. P. Dananjaya
AU  - N. L. P. I. Candrawengi
AU  - I. W. A. Suranata
AU  - I. G. N. D. Paramartha
T2  - 2025 IEEE 2nd International Conference on Cryptography, Informatics, and Cybersecurity (ICoCICs)
AB  - In contemporary network infrastructures, the implementation of Secure Shell (SSH) security measures is of paramount importance. However, the system's vulnerability to sophisticated attacks, including brute force attacks and configuration anomalies, is a matter of concern. The timely identification of these sources of danger is hindered by the abundance of log data and existing class imbalances. In this study, the performance of three sequential deep learning models—Long Short-Term Memory, Gated Recurrent Unit, and 1-Dimensional Convolutional Neural Network—for detecting anomalies in SSH logs is evaluated and compared. A synthetic log dataset was created to encompass both normal behavior and various types of attacks. This was preprocessed using temporal feature engineering and examined for class imbalances using class weighting. The evaluation process, which utilizes a dedicated test set, has revealed that all models exhibit a high degree of precision in detecting brute force attacks. The GRU model had the highest F1 score and accuracy. The 1D CNN had the best recall rate for regular traffic but the lowest precision. This study thoroughly compared three deep learning models: LSTM, GRU, and 1D CNN. This benchmarking used a new synthetic SSH log dataset to simulate real-world challenges, like extreme class imbalance and rare configuration anomalies. The analysis shows each model's strengths and weaknesses in this context. This study highlights the challenge of detecting brute-force attacks while minimizing false positives. Current anomaly detection methods are limited when it comes to extremely rare patterns.
