T2  - 2025 Thirteenth International Conference on Advanced Cloud and Big Data (CBD)
AB  - Log-based anomaly detection is one of the most prevalent and effective means for maintaining software security. However, existing methods are challenged by the continuous influx of log data. The distribution of log data may evolve over time, and labeling the large-scale incoming data is both time-consuming and labor-intensive. To address these issues, we propose SIGLog, a semi-supervised log anomaly detection update method based on time series generation, which comprises two modules: a semi-supervised classifier and a conditional time series generator. The classifier integrates a hybrid architecture that effectively models temporal dependencies, while refining feature representations through a class-separation loss objective. The conditional generator, Time-CVAE, synthesizes label-conditioned log sequences to facilitate online replay. Extensive experiments demonstrate that under conditions of limited labeled data and evolving data distribution, SIGLog shows better performance across major evaluation metrics of log anomaly detection and maintains stable performance even as the proportion of labeled data decreases.
