TI  - Performance Evaluation of LLMs for Generating and Enriching Application Behavioural Automata
AU  - K. Isobe
AU  - A. Urli
AU  - N. Schnepf
AU  - R. Badonnel
T2  - NOMS 2026-2026 IEEE Network Operations and Management Symposium
AB  - The Internet growth is characterized by the multiplication and sophistication of security attacks, that are leveraged by Artificial Intelligence (AI) techniques. In the meantime, networks are expected to become increasingly AI-native. In particular, security automation enhanced by AI techniques, including Large Language Models (LLMs), will enable a better orchestration and adaptation of security functions to counter these attacks efficiently. In this paper, we propose a testbed architecture for evaluating the performance of LLMs to generate and enrich probabilistic automata characterizing the network behaviour of end device applications. These behavioural automata can then serve as a support to build and configure chains of security functions, that are deployed in network infrastructures. The testbed architecture consists of three main pipelines. The first pipeline specifies a baseline method based on process mining to generate behavioural automata. The second pipeline corresponds to the generation of automata based on LLM techniques. The third pipeline combines the two previous ones. It first generates automata using process mining, and then augments them using LLM techniques. Based on the prototyping of this testbed architecture, we have performed extensive series of experiments to evaluate the performances of LLM techniques with respect to the generation and enrichment of automata, and also to quantify their level of explainability in that context.
