AU  - B. S. Srinivas
T2  - 2026 9th International Conference on Inventive Computation Technologies (ICICT)
AB  - Hospital information systems produce multiple audit trails that are high in volume, but the detection of breaches is reactive and rule-based. The current paper suggests a hybrid system of temporal anomaly detectors, which offers a combination of behavioural sequence encoding, cross-system log fusion, and attribution-oriented anomaly marking to early breach identification. It learns temporal embeddings in a gated sequence encoder on 148 million EHR access events and 32 million authentication events of two tertiary hospitals. The architecture emits an event stream anomaly score and is supported by an attribution head (finding causal access tokens). The model has an AUROC of 0.964 and a macro-F1 score of 0.781, which is better than rule-based SIEM (+0.112 AUROC) and LSTM baselines (+0.067 AUROC), on a 1:920 breach-to-normal ratio. The detection latency mean is 38ms per session and false positive rates are equally reduced by 27 percent at the same level of recall.
