TI  - Adaptive Cyber Threat Detection Using Event-Aware LSTM Networks
AU  - A. V. Ramana
AU  - C. Pallavi
AU  - M. Gangadhar
AU  - B. S. U. Kumar
AU  - B. Lochan
AU  - U. K. Priya
T2  - 2026 4th International Conference on Knowledge Engineering and Communication Systems (ICKECS)
AB  - Traditional signature-based IDS is no longer sufficient due to the sophistication of cyberattacks and increased network traffic. DL holds immense promise for automatic threat detection; however, existing models face difficulties in real-world dynamic scenarios. There still exist two major challenges: we cannot model irregular time intervals in network logs, and performance degradation due to concept drift when attack patterns and normal traffic change over time.This report proposes a strong framework for Adaptive Cyber Threat Detection using Event-Aware LSTM Networks. This model captures variable time gaps and better detects low-and-slow attacks with a time-decay mechanism, which is different from traditional methods assuming events are equidistant in time. The model is then combined with Event2Vec embedding to encode various log event types better than one-hot representations. An online drift detection module based on the ADWIN algorithm monitors the model’s performance and initiates its retraining in incremental steps during distributional shifts. This keeps the model adaptive over time. The experiments with CICIDS2017, UNSW-NB15 and simulated drift scenarios prove that the Event-Aware LSTM can achieve an accuracy of up to 99.5% and quickly adapts after a concept drift, hence is less vulnerable compared to the static DL models. This work enhances self-adjusting autonomous SIEM systems that would work dependably in cyber-physical surroundings that are unstable.
