AU  - G. Yaqiang
AU  - M. Jialin
AU  - L. Shuo
AU  - S. Yu
T2  - 2025 IEEE 8th International Conference on Automation, Electronics and Electrical Engineering (AUTEEE)
AB  - To address the challenges of traditional power network security detection technologies, such as weak adaptability to dynamic topologies, poor fusion of multi-source heterogeneous data, and high false alarm rates, this paper proposes a dynamic weighted heterogeneous graph convolutional network (DW-HGCN). This algorithm unifies the feature scales of device status, network traffic, and security log data through a heterogeneous data input layer. A dynamic weight calculation module adapts to topology changes by combining device importance and real-time threat severity. An improved graph convolutional layer enhances spatiotemporal coupling feature extraction and redundant information suppression. Experiments on a dataset containing 100,000 data points (public and provincial power data), demonstrated that the DW-HGCN achieved a threat detection accuracy of 98.2%, an improvement of 4.5 and 8.3 percentage points over GAT (93.7%) and SVM (89.9%), respectively. The inference latency was 0.32 seconds, meeting the real-time requirement of ≤0.5 seconds. The false alarm rate and missed alarm rate were 1.1% and 1.3%, respectively, reducing the false alarm rate by 2.1-6.8 percentage points compared to competing algorithms. Ablation experiments show that removing dynamic weights or improving the graph convolutional layer results in a 3.1 and 1.1 percentage point decrease in accuracy, respectively, validating the effectiveness of the core module. This algorithm provides an efficient solution for power network security protection.
