TI  - Anomaly-Based Detection of Insider Threats Using Network Traffic Behaviour
AU  - S. Kakarla
AU  - U. S.
AU  - S. G
T2  - 2026 International Conference on Emerging Technologies in Computing and Communication (ETCC)
AB  - Insider threats are a very serious danger to the security of enterprises because adversarial action can be masked as normal user activity. Consequently, there is a great deal of difficulty in using conventional signature or rule-based detection methods to identify deviances from established norms that would show malicious actions. This paper presents a framework for anomaly-based detection of insider threats that examines user email activity in order to define what “normal” email communication looks like for the organization as a whole. To find deviations from established norms, behaviours are extracted based upon individual users and across time. Anomalous user behaviours are then analyzed and classified into one of three categories - low, medium, or high risk - using unsupervised machine learning techniques such as an Isolation Forest classifier. Using these classifications to calculate risk scores for each user, security analysts are provided an interactive visualization of user behaviours, allowing them to monitor for anomalous user behaviours. The experimental results of applying the proposed method to detect potential insider threats using no labeled data has been validated using enterprise email log data. The results of an empirical evaluation of the proposed unsupervised framework demonstrated the ability of the framework to prioritize suspicious user behaviours; of the $\mathbf{1, 0 0 0}$ users monitored in the CERT® Insider Threat Dataset, there were four classified as high risk.
