AU  - S. Abid
AU  - U. Abid
AU  - M. O. Malik
AU  - F. Azam
T2  - 2025 International Conference on Electrical, Communication and Computer Engineering (ICECCE)
AB  - Anomaly detection in large-scale log data has become increasingly critical for ensuring complex systems' security, reliability, and operational efficiency. Traditional rulebased and statistical techniques often fall short in handling modern log environments' high dimensionality, noise, and scalability requirements. This study presents a simulation-based anomaly detection framework employing deep autoencoders to learn latent representations of normal log patterns and detect deviations via reconstruction loss. Synthetic log datasets were generated to mimic real-world formats, with various injected anomalies such as unauthorized access patterns, spikes, and missing entries. A dynamic thresholding mechanism based on statistical methods, including the interquartile range (IQR), was used to enhance the detection accuracy. Extensive simulations demonstrated that the proposed model achieves an F1-score of 0.908 and an AUC of 0.963 while maintaining low latency, indicating its potential for real-time deployment in large-scale systems. The results affirm that autoencoder-based approaches offer a scalable and effective solution for log-based anomaly detection.
