TI  - A Linux Kernel Anomaly Detection Based on a Multi-Source eBPF Observability Pipeline and Unsupervised Autoencoders
AU  - D. M. Mukristu
AU  - D. Tshimbalanga Kazumba
AU  - A. K. Lusala
AU  - P. S. Nzakuna
AU  - M. Palladino
AU  - V. Paciello
T2  - 2026 IEEE International Symposium on Measurement in Networking and Communications (MNC)
AB  - Linux systems underpin a large share of modern digital infrastructures, yet their internal behavior remains difficult to characterize using conventional user-space monitoring alone. Many abnormal situations do not appear as explicit signatures, but rather as deviations from nominal system behavior. This paper presents a kernel-level behavioral observability and anomaly detection pipeline that combines eBPF-based instrumentation with unsupervised learning. Multiple eBPF probes are used to capture heterogeneous low-level events related to CPU activity, process execution, process lifecycle, network interactions, and privilege-related operations. These asynchronous events are then merged, synchronized, and aggregated into fixed-size temporal windows, producing a homogeneous multivariate representation of host behavior. The resulting dataset is modeled using three autoencoder variants: a standard dense autoencoder (AE), a denoising autoencoder (DAE), and a sparse autoencoder (SAE). All models are trained on data assumed to represent nominal system operation, and anomaly detection is performed through reconstruction error thresholding based on a high validation quantile. The experimental dataset was built from multiple observation sessions totaling approximately 3 h 45 min of nominal activity and represented by 13,641 temporal windows described by 36 input features. Results show that reconstruction-error distributions remain largely concentrated below their learned thresholds, with only 6, 5, and 5 candidate anomalous windows detected on the test set for AE, DAE, and SAE, respectively, out of 2,047 windows. Moreover, five test windows were detected jointly by all three models, and their dominant contributing variables were associated with coherent process, network, CPU, and privilege-related behaviors. These results support the feasibility of combining eBPF-based kernel observability with autoencoder-based unsupervised modeling for behavioral anomaly detection in Linux environments.
