AU  - C. Huaman
AU  - D. Gianfranco
AU  - D. Alarcón
AU  - J. Stuwart
T2  - 2025 35th International Conference on Computer Theory and Applications (ICCTA)
AB  - In recent years, information security has gained critical importance, driven by an alarming surge in cyberattacks. In Peru alone, more than 15 billion attempts have been reported—a trend that continues to rise. Enterprise networks are among the most affected, particularly in critical sectors such as healthcare, where the protection of information is essential. In this context, a hybrid AI-based detection and mitigation model is proposed that is designed to deliver a lightweight, efficient, and low-cost solution capable of automatically identifying and responding to threats in real time without compromising network performance. Network traffic is analyzed on a Raspberry Pi 5 using Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) and Long Short-Term Memory (LSTMs), with tools such as TensorFlow/Keras, Flask, SQLite, and iptables. The architecture is tailored for resource-constrained environments, facilitating deployment in real-world networks. The methodology was structured into five phases: tool selection, preparation of the CICIDS2017 dataset, model training, integration in a controlled environment, and validation at a hospital in Lima. An accuracy of 97% was achieved, providing a solid foundation for future real-world deployments.
