TI  - Secure Organization Risk Audit Tool (SORAT): A Comprehensive Deep Learning and Explainable AI Framework for Enterprise Risk Classification
AU  - J. Praveenkumar
AU  - O. Pandidurai
AU  - G. Saikrishnan
AU  - O. K.
AU  - A. M. Jenson
T2  - 2026 International Conference on System, Computation, Automation and Networking (ICSCAN)
AB  - Companies today have firewalls and login systems etc that throw data at them. Manually sifting through all of this is almost impossible resulting in missed threats, staff working on too many alerts and inconsistent responses. Older rulebased systems are good at identifying known attacks but struggle with false positives and new threats. This paper introduces the Secure Organisation Risk Audit Tool (SORAT) that utilises deep learning to classify security logs into one of five levels of risk, and provides a simple English rationale behind the classification. SORAT does this using a five layer convolutional neural network, gaussian normalisation and other fancy tricks. They tested it on the UNSW-NB15 dataset to see how good it is. They made a few dummy ones to get it to 8500. The results are quite good, achieving 94.8% accuracy, 93.8% F1-score and AUC of 0.96 in cross-validation tests. SORAT is quick, but it's not just quick. It can process 2800 events sequences per second making real time use of SOC very probable. It also has a FastAPI REST interface for easy integration with existing systems and uses heatmaps to accelerate analyst triage. This means that SORAT has the potential to change the way to do security log analysis and improve the threat management in general.
