TY  - JOUR
TI  - SMATA: A Structured Mobile Application Testing Architecture Adapting Hardware Verification Principles for Mobile Testing Standardization
AU  - S. Elsayed
AU  - K. Azhar Aziz
T2  - IEEE Access
AB  - Mobile application development organizations face persistent quality assurance challenges due to the absence of standardized testing architectures. Teams routinely construct ad hoc testing infrastructures for each project, incurring repeated setup overhead, inconsistent coverage, and poor bug reproducibility. Inspired by the Universal Verification Methodology (UVM) from hardware verification, we propose SMATA (the Structured Mobile Application Testing Architecture), a modular, reusable framework that unifies disparate testing tools through standardized interfaces, automates complex initialization flows, and systematically captures events to enable fault reproduction. Building on prior work in reusable hardware accelerator architectures, similar principles of modularity and structured design can be extended beyond hardware into software testing infrastructures. We evaluate SMATA across 50 open-source Android applications and 20 iOS applications spanning seven domains and 2K–95K lines of code, guided by five explicit research questions. Compared to Monkey, Dynodroid, and ad hoc baselines, SMATA achieves 64.2% average statement coverage (a 48.9% relative improvement over the ad hoc baseline and approximately 18% over Maestro on a 10-app subset;  $p \lt 0.001$ ,  $d \gt 1.0$ ), 88.7% bug reproducibility ( $3.93\times $  over Monkey), 64.8% fault detection rate via mutation testing, and 88.9% setup-time reduction when reusing components across projects. An ablation study confirms that each SMATA component makes a measurable independent contribution. Initial cross-platform results on iOS show less than 3% coverage delta relative to Android, providing early evidence of architectural generalizability; a larger-scale validation is required for definitive conclusions. All experimental artifacts and prototype source code are publicly available at https://github.com/saher-elsayed/smata
