TY  - JOUR
TI  - LLM-LogHGC: Hypergraph Enhanced Unsupervised Log Parsing With Lightweight LLMs
AU  - D. Wu
AU  - J. Xu
T2  - IEEE Transactions on Network and Service Management
AB  - Log parsing converts log messages into structured data, serving as a critical step in log analysis. Traditional log parsers rely on predefined rules or on trainable models that require labeled data. Although they are effective in particular settings, they exhibit limited generalizability and struggle to handle complex log data. Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have enabled LLM-based log parsers to achieve outstanding performance. However, existing LLM-based parsers exhibit limited sensitivity to structural information, and pose privacy risks when using commercial models. Log data naturally exhibits pronounced structural patterns, and its intrinsic regularities implicitly encode log template information, enabling lightweight LLMs to parse it accurately. In this paper, we propose LLM-LogHGC, a log parsing framework enhanced with hypergraph structures, deployable on lightweight open-source LLMs. Without requiring manually labeled logs for contextual learning or fine-tuning, it leverages the hypergraph structure to capture latent relationships and similarities between logs to assist parsing. Specifically, LLM-LogHGC models each log message as a hyperedge, with the tokens in the message serving as nodes, thereby constructing the log hypergraph. Logs are preliminarily partitioned based on hyperedge connectivity. Subsequently, LLM-LogHGC uses a specially designed Masked Attention Hypergraph Convolutional Network to learn hyperedge embeddings and compute their similarities. Finally, similar log messages are input into the LLM for parsing. We introduce a template cache to reduce LLM queries and a structural verification mechanism to mitigate LLM-induced errors, improving parsing efficiency and accuracy. Comprehensive experiments on public datasets demonstrate that LLM-LogHGC outperforms state-of-the-art methods.
