TI  - Process over Product: A Real-Time, Big Data Approach to Plagiarism Detection via Activity Logs
AU  - B. S. A. Rao
AU  - S. Gurram
AU  - J. Carvalho
AU  - A. P S
AU  - A. Giri
T2  - 2026 IEEE Global Engineering Education Conference (EDUCON)
AB  - The rise of generative Artificial Intelligence (AI) tools has introduced new challenges in plagiarism detection within computer science education and technical recruitment. Traditional plagiarism detectors, which rely on pairwise code similarity, are increasingly ineffective against AI-generated or heavily obfuscated submissions. This paper presents a distributed system that detects plagiarism by monitoring the process of code creation rather than the final output. The system captures keystroke activity logs from students’ web-based editors and performs real-time anomaly detection using a distributed event streaming and processing pipeline built on Apache Kafka, Apache Spark, and vector similarity search. An Isolation Forest-based machine learning model is employed to identify suspicious behavioral patterns indicative of code plagiarism. A teacher-facing dashboard provides real-time alerts and analytics for active assignments. The system was evaluated through both synthetic scalability testing and an in-class deployment involving real students. The in-class evaluation yielded a precision of 93.67% and a recall of 90.24%, illustrating that process-level behavioral analysis can reliably detect plagiarism attempts in live, adversarial educational settings. These results indicate that such behavioral monitoring can serve as a practical complement to existing similarity-based plagiarism detection methods.
