TY  - CONF
TI  - Time Warping Hybrid Modeling for Case Arrivals in Business Process Simulation
AU  - G. Nespoulous
AU  - F. Bertrand
AU  - M. Maumy
AU  - Y. Valero
T2  - 2026 3rd International Conference on Connected Innovation and Technology (ICCITX)
AB  - Faithful simulation of case arrivals is critical for Business Process Simulation (BPS), but in practice, real-world process arrivals rarely conform to i.i.d. renewal assumptions. At a macro level, case arrivals follow calendar seasonality and non-stationary trends. At the micro level, they are affected by short-range dependencies and burstiness that come from work resource limitations, operational policies, etc. These two dynamics could be represented jointly using coupled Cox-Hawkes models, but in practice, they are hard to calibrate, and long-horizon simulations are expensive. Our primary contribution is a modular method that enables a macro-micro model of case arrivals that retains the macro seasonality without erasing residual short-range micro structure. We model the calendar seasonality before using a Doob-Meyer time-rescaling transformation that maps arrivals into a warped time representation. In this domain, we train a micro model that models deviations from a Poisson baseline to represent the residual micro-level structure. The generated timestamps are then unwarped using the inverse transform, recovering, by construction, the macro seasonality. Experiments on real event logs with minute-level granularity and batched arrivals show that with only minor degradation in macro-level representation, the micro-level fidelity is meaningfully improved. While computationally light and suited for practical BPS, the presented method yields more realistic arrival micro-patterns that underlie congestion-sensitive KPIs.
