AB  - To address issues such as the dynamic variations of large-scale log data, the difficulty in obtaining anomaly labels, and time-series dependencies, a log data anomaly detection method based on incremental contrastive learning is proposed. This method employs incremental contrastive learning to train data to adapt to continuously changing data distributions and the problem of lacking labeled data. It utilizes a distillation approach to design the loss function, transferring knowledge from the original model to the new model to mitigate catastrophic forgetting issues. In the sequence data processing stage, an attention shift mechanism is introduced to automatically learn the importance of each feature for the detection model, shifting the model’s focus to the most informative features to enhance the accuracy of detection. Experiments conducted on open-source datasets validate that this method effectively improves the accuracy of anomaly detection.
