TI  - Event-Based Optical Tactile Sensor and Sparse Transformer for Robust Snap-Fit Recognition
AU  - B. Cao
AU  - S. Fang
AU  - Z. Lin
AU  - M. Zeng
AU  - Z. Yi
T2  - 2025 IEEE International Conference on Robotics and Biomimetics (ROBIO)
AB  - Snap-fit assembly enables rapid fastening via elastic deformation of components and is widely used in electronics and automotive manufacturing. As a contact-rich task, it demands extremely high positional accuracy and slight force deviations or improper force application can easily lead to assembly failure. Traditional force/torque (F/T) sensors cannot capture connector poses, while normal optical tactile sensors, limited by insufficient sampling rates, struggle to capture the short-term deformation and rebound of components during the snap-fit process. To address this issue, this paper proposes a snap-fit state recognition framework based on an event-based tactile sensor. The framework converts event streams into sequences of spatiotemporal event frames using the Time Surface method, then classifies snap-fit assembly states via a Transformer network integrating sparse tokens and learnable memory vectors. We built a robotic platform to collect snap-fit datasets for Type-C, Ethernet, and FPC connectors to validate the effectiveness of the framework. Experiments show that the framework performs excellently in all datasets-particularly on the FPC dataset, achieving an F1-score 10% higher than other models. This validates the effectiveness of the event-based optical tactile sensor and provides a new solution for contactrich robotic assembly tasks.
