T2  - 2026 6th International Conference on Computer Communication and Artificial Intelligence (CCAI)
AB  - To mitigate the high operational workload caused by the dispersion of multi-source information and information overload on the ship bridge, and to address the lack of forward-looking decision references in emergency scenarios, this study develops a navigation assistance decision-making system that provides navigational recommendations within a future time window. A message-driven architecture is constructed based on a Data Distribution Service (DDS) message bus to enable unified access and standardization of multi-source sensor data. The system performs per-second data updates and alarm triggering, and continuously generates rolling navigational decision recommendations for a predefined future time window. In addition, mechanisms for critical data source disconnection detection and event logging are implemented. A system-level process watchdog is introduced to ensure automatic recovery within seconds in the event of process failures. Real-ship experiments demonstrate that the system can consistently outputs alarms and decision recommendations and decision recommendations. Per-second alarms are generated upon data source disconnection, and abnormal processes are automatically restored within seconds. The proposed system achieves multi-source data integration, time-window-based navigational recommendation output, and high-availability self-recovery capabilities. These features significantly enhance the real-time performance and reliability of navigation assistance decision-making, indicating strong potential for practical engineering applications.
