TI  - Real-Time Log Intelligence Framework with Explainable Machine Learning for Automated Root Cause Analysis in Distributed Systems
AU  - R. Murugadoss
AU  - P. M
AU  - P. N
AU  - E. V
T2  - 2026 International Conference on Data Science for Cyber-Physical Systems Resilience using Advanced Applications (ICDCA)
AB  - The amount of unstructured logs generated by distributed and cloud-native systems is enormous and can include many millions of entries per minute; therefore, it is nearly impossible to manually analyze the logs in real-time and it is also slow to detect faults and cause of downtime in systems. The following paper introduces a Real-Time Log Intelligence Framework that is based on using Machine Learning to identify anomalous conditions and identify the root causes of failures using raw log data. The framework uses a Bi-LSTM (Bidirectional Long Short Term Memory) network with Temporal Logical Attention Mechanism to acquire intricate failure pattern by raw logs without defining the rules. The model, in contrast to the common black-box models, also provides Explainable AI, by displaying the weight of attention on key events in the logs that are responsible to failures. These experiments conducted on the HDFS benchmark dataset led to the successful achievement of the following values of Accuracy (98.2 percent), Precision (96.8 percent) and Recall (95.4 percent) which is better than the results of the traditional Machine Learning Methods and the Standard LSTM Models. The proposed system will provide a reduction in Mean Time To Resolve (MTTR), and hence provide more rapid and understandable faults detection of distributed systems.
