TI  - Event-Driven Traffic Congestion Prediction Based on Multimodal Dataset
AU  - W. Yang
AU  - H. Xie
AU  - H. Xie
AU  - D. Lian
T2  - 2025 11th International Conference on Computer and Communications (ICCC)
AB  - Accurate prediction of urban traffic congestion is vital for improving traffic management efficiency. However, most existing models focus on overall traffic flow and show limited effectiveness in event-driven congestion scenarios due to complex event-congestion causality and the difficulty of integrating temporal traffic data with event information. This study investigates the spatiotemporal correlations between various event types and congestion using the multimodal BjTT dataset, which contains both traffic flow and event records. Then we propose MMSTGCN, a dual-branch multimodal spatiotemporal graph convolutional network that processes traffic flow and event data separately, fuses them via gated recurrent units, and captures spatiotemporal dependencies as well as latent causal relationships. Experimental results on BjTT demonstrate that MMSTGCN achieves an F1score of 0.7518 and precision of 0.8089, outperforming mainstream baselines. Compared with the STGCN+CA model that also incorporates event data but uses different fusion strategies, MMSTGCN achieves relative improvements of 15.24% in F1score and 36.11% in precision. These results confirm that deep integration of multimodal data, particularly event information, into continuous spatiotemporal prediction frameworks significantly enhances event-driven congestion prediction and provides valuable support for intelligent urban traffic management.
