TI  - Deep Learning-Based Approaches for Insider Threat Detection in Cybersecurity
AU  - A. Alfalahi
AU  - A. Panthakkan
AU  - H. A. Hamadi
T2  - 2026 Student Multidisciplinary Innovation and Research Conference (SMIRC)
AB  - Insider threats remain one of the most difficult cybersecurity problems because they often involve trusted users whose actions appear legitimate at first. These threats may result from negligence, malicious intent, or compromised credentials, making them difficult to detect using traditional rule-based systems. Such systems usually depend on fixed thresholds and predefined patterns, which limits their ability to recognize subtle changes in user behavior, especially when labeled insider threat data are limited. This paper reviews recent studies that apply deep learning techniques to insider threat detection, including long short-term memory networks, autoencoders, transformers, convolutional models, and graph neural networks. The review shows a clear movement from static monitoring methods toward behavior-based and context-aware detection. It also discusses multimodal approaches that combine digital activity logs with video anomaly detection to provide stronger security insights. However, several challenges remain, including limited realistic datasets, high false-positive rates, weak model interpretability, privacy concerns, and real-time deployment constraints. To address these issues, this paper proposes a multimodal deep learning framework that combines access-log sequence analysis, edge-based video anomaly detection, and organizational context modeling. The proposed framework is intended to improve detection accuracy, reduce unnecessary alerts, and support privacyaware real-time insider threat detection.
