AB  - The recent fast development of the Large Language Models (LLMs) has provided some major opportunities to automate and optimize the work of the Security Operations Centers (SOCs) activity. This paper discusses the potential transformational aspect of LLMs in enhancing the efficiency of the cyber security defensive measures, such as the creation of threat intelligence, automatic threat event tent rage, logic management approach and adversarial behavior detection. Since the capabilities of the natural language processor are used in models like GPT-4 among others, SOC teams have gained an opportunity to improve rapid response, burnout of curb analysts, and, accordingly, simplified modalities of choice. However, there are also varied technical, operational, and ethical threats tied to the use of the LLM with major security infrastructure. These are model hallucination, adversarial prompting, data leakage, as well as overreliance on opaque outputs. The article provides a critical evaluation of such dualities, charting the advantages of AI-enhanced defense in terms of its possible flaws. The hybrid human-AI structure is suggested as a necessary measure to guarantee prosperous monitoring, transparency and flexibility in the SOC environments. The results support the need to exercise tremendous care during deployment, thoroughly analyze, and harmonize policies to ensure the maximum value of LLMs in the cyber security operation today.
