AU  - G. R. Afdholy
AU  - J. Ari Purnama
AU  - N. S. Sitohang
T2  - 2025 International Conference on Technology and Policy in Energy and Electric Power (ICT-PEP)
AB  - Maintaining high reliability within electricity distribution networks has become increasingly challenging due to rising energy demands, the proliferation of distributed energy resources (DERs), and complex operational environments. In West Sumatera, Indonesia, these challenges are further amplified by geographical constraints and aging infrastructure. To address these issues, a smart grid-based Fault Indicator (FI) self-healing system was deployed, with the Padang Koto Gadang feeder serving as the pioneering site. This paper presents the design, implementation, and performance evaluation of the system, which integrates real-time fault detection, automatic fault isolation, and predictive maintenance strategies. Additionally, an innovative robotic communication layer utilizing instant mobile messaging platforms was incorporated to enhance field crew responsiveness. Operational results demonstrated substantial improvements: a 87.2% reduction in the System Average Interruption Duration Index (SAIDI), an 92.3% decrease in Energy Not Served (ENS), and a significant acceleration of service restoration times. A comparative evaluation against a conventional feeder operating under manual procedures further validated the effectiveness of the smart self-healing approach. The successful deployment in Padang Koto Gadang led to the rapid expansion of the system across the entire West Sumatera distribution network, offering a replicable model for modernizing active distribution grids toward enhanced resilience and customer-centric operation.
