AU  - H. Maeda
AU  - S. Sannomiya
AU  - K. Kameyama
AU  - H. Takizawa
AU  - M. Iwata
AU  - H. Nishikawa
T2  - 2025 IEEE 15th Annual Computing and Communication Workshop and Conference (CCWC)
AB  - This paper presents an external and lightweight monitoring circuit for detecting anomalies such as cyberattacks and failures in computing platforms. An external monitoring is preferable because of its natural immunity to anomalies in target platforms and its little consumption of the platforms' processing power. However, existing monitoring methods require a processor-scale additional circuit and thus reducing circuit size still remains a challenge, especially for resource-limited Internet of Things (IoT) devices. To achieve not only external but also lightweight monitoring, we adopt a self-timed data-driven processor (STDDP) and exploit its unique characteristic. STDDP executes a process independently from the other processes and the execution time of a process is kept the shortest and constant as long as its computational resources are adequately available. We innovatively make the number of processes in execution (NPE) externally monitorable as the criterion of anomaly detection. The constant process execution time leads to a precise sampling of the actual NPE value without distinction of process. It also enables us to algebraically calculate the normal value of NPE by using the execution times of corresponding processes as well as target data sets' values and input timing. The discrepancy between the sampled and normal values of NPE absolutely indicates the existence of malicious or unexpected processes spawned by the anomalies. Experimental results show that an anomaly detection can be realized with an additional circuit of less than one-third of the processor-scale size.
