TI  - Smart Wearable IoT System for Early Stroke Detection and Remote Patient Monitoring using LoRaWAN
AU  - S. P. Santhoshkumar
AU  - C. Mariyammal
AU  - A. Yuvarani
AU  - U. R. K
AU  - D. Vikram
AU  - P. Arora
T2  - 2026 4th International Conference on Intelligent Cyber Physical Systems and Internet of Things (ICoICI)
AB  - Stroke is one of the most common causes of death and chronic neurological disability in the world and needs prompt diagnosis, systematic screening of patients for optimal medical management. Hospital-based monitoring systems are frequently not suitable for continuous monitoring, especially in areas with limited resources and access. To overcome these shortcomings, this paper proposes a novel, advanced IoT-based real-time stroke detection system as well as an emergency alert system using LoRaWAN communication technology. The proposed system involves the use of wearable biomedical sensors which continuously capture important physiological parameters, such as heart rate, blood pressure, blood oxygen saturation (SpO2) and body movements. The data gathered from the sensors are preprocessed and analysed using intelligent threshold based method to detect any abnormal physiological conditions related to the possible occurrence of a stroke event. Data from this process is sent securely to the cloud-based healthcare platform using LoRaWAN (long-range technology and low power) and is thus monitored, stored and managed in real time in the event of emergency. Automated alerts are sent to healthcare professionals and caregivers to facilitate quick clinical action in case of critical abnormalities occurring. The prototype system was realized with the use of ESP32 controllers, LoRa transceivers, and sensing modules integrated in the system. Experimental results show that the proposed framework can accurately detect the changing environment with a low communication latency and optimal energy consumption, achieving an accuracy of about 94%. The developed system is scalable, reliable, and cost-efficient which would be suitable for intelligent healthcare monitoring and early diagnosis of stroke remotely.
