AU  - B. Li
AU  - S. Lu
AU  - N. Han
AU  - X. Wei
AU  - W. Lu
T2  - 2025 IEEE Smart World Congress (SWC)
AB  - While recent advances in deep learning have led to lightweight models capable of performing automatic log analysis with high accuracy, the emergence of large language models (LLMs) has opened up new possibilities in this domain. However, these studies often focus only on improving performance and robustness, overlooking real-world industrial scenarios. In addition, these studies vary greatly in terms of model complexity, training data volume, and fine-tuning methods, making it difficult to conduct a fair comparison and highlighting the need for standardized evaluation metrics. In this paper, we propose a hierarchical LoRA fine-tuning strategy that enables LLMs to efficiently adapt to log data and perform related analysis tasks with improved parameter efficiency. Additionally, we introduce a comprehensive set of evaluation metrics tailored for log analysis with LLMs, designed to evaluate the performance of LLM-based log analysis from multiple perspectives in a fair manner. Through extensive experiments on publicly available log datasets, we demonstrate that our fine-tuning approach achieves a favorable balance between downstream performance and memory overhead. We further validate the practicality of our proposed evaluation framework by evaluating a range of existing LLM-based log analysis approaches.
