TI  - Generative AI Optimization of Enterprise SIEM & Confidential Logging Infrastructure
AU  - V. v. U. K. Jonnalagadda
T2  - 2025 International Conference on Advanced Technologies and Interdisciplinary Innovation (ICAT2I)
AB  - Enterprise-wide systems such as Splunk, IBM QRadar, and Elastic SIEM are fundamental to detect, monitor and analyze cybersecurity events in real-time. The classic SIEM existing in the market has increased challenges in processing big, fast, and great size of log data created by heterogeneous data sources. In GenAI-powered long agricultural systems, this study aims to propose a framework to optimize log analysis, correlation, and detecting threats in enterprise SIEM systems. The framework uses the generative transformer-VAE hybrid model based on privacy accuracy and data utility across privacy models to automatically link events, recreate missing logs, and synthesize data to be used and trained to perform anomaly detection modules; this is done all without compromising data confidentiality via differential privacy and homomorphic encryption. Multi-platform SIEM data evaluations demonstrate experimental improvements in accuracy when detecting, delivering the required log correlation speed, and false positive reduction after it is compared with the standard SIEM dynamics powered by adaptability and policy update frequency in AI-based privacy systems. Along with such advantages, there are still drawbacks like model interpretability, query latency comparison and cloud database privacy models tuning of methods in heterogeneous settings. Future research areas involve federated generative SIEM systems, mobile GenAI to generate edge nodes in SIEM, and an AI to predict and generate just-in-time correlation to assist adaptable and private capability to address threats in an enterprise scale.
