TI  - LogKDR: A Knowledge-Distilled Retrieval-Augmented Framework for Interpretable Log Analysis
AU  - J. Liu
AU  - J. Qin
AU  - X. Han
AU  - D. Du
AU  - Y. Zhu
AU  - B. Jiang
AU  - Z. Lu
T2  - 2026 29th International Conference on Computer Supported Cooperative Work in Design (CSCWD)
AB  - As a vital instrument for recording system and device operational status, logs have been widely adopted across various information systems. Log anomaly detection and root cause analysis are crucial for ensuring the reliability and security of large-scale software systems. However, current deep learning-based approaches suffer from limited interpretability, while methods relying on pre-trained models face challenges such as high computational costs and privacy concerns. To address these limitations, we propose LogKDR, a Knowledge Distilled Retrieval-Augmented framework that integrates Multi-hop Retrieval(MHR), Knowledge Distillation(kD), and Self-adaptive Active Learning(SAAL) into a unified closed-loop system. The framework introduces three key innovations: MHR constructs hierarchical contextual evidence chains across heterogeneous sources, enabling fine-grained correlation from symptoms to root causes; KD transfers reasoning capabilities from a high-capacity teacher model to a lightweight student model for efficient on-device inference; and SAAL ensures continuous model evolution under concept drift through uncertainty-driven pseudo-labeling and incremental updates. Experiments on three benchmark datasets—HDFS, BGL, and Thunderbird—demonstrate that LogKDR achieves an average improvement of 18.12% in F1-score over large models while significantly enhancing interpretability metrics(ROUGE and BLEU), attaining state-of-the-art performance. Ablation studies confirm that each component critically contributes to the framework’s robustness, efficiency, and explainability in dynamic log environments.
