TY  - CONF
TI  - Scalable Anomaly Detection in Cloud Logs with Sparse Transformer
AU  - A. V.R.
T2  - 2025 IEEE Recent Advances in Intelligent Computational Systems (RAICS)
AB  - Log anomaly detection plays a crucial role in the analysis of cloud log files by identifying irregular log entries that may signify system faults or security breaches. With the growing complexity of modern computing systems, traditional manual analysis methods have become increasingly impractical and time-intensive. Consequently, recent advancements have focused on leveraging machine learning and artificial intelligence techniques for automated log anomaly detection. Among these, transformer-based models grounded in natural language processing have demonstrated promising results. However, their effectiveness can be limited when dealing with highly unstructured log data or when anomalous events are distantly located within the logs. This limitation is primarily due to the quadratic computational complexity of standard transformers with respect to input sequence length. To overcome this challenge, sparse transformer architectures have emerged, offering sub-quadratic complexity and improved scalability. In this study, we propose a sparse transformer-based approach for analyzing cloud log data, aimed at enhancing anomaly detection and supporting evidence acquisition in cloud forensic investigations. The proposed sparse transformer-based model is evaluated using annotated benchmark datasets, with performance assessed primarily through F1-scores and the estimated probability density functions of anomaly scores. The experimental results demonstrate that the sparse transformer model consistently outperforms two other baseline models in terms of F1-score across multiple datasets, indicating that the ability to process larger input sequences contributes positively to anomaly detection performance.
