TY  - CONF
TI  - AI-Driven System Design for Human-Animal Conflict Mitigation
AU  - S. A. K. C
AU  - K. K
AU  - M.K.Elango
T2  - 2025 9th International Conference on Electronics, Communication and Aerospace Technology (ICECA)
AB  - The occurrence of human-animal conflicts within agricultural landscapes is a major threat to food security and livelihood as well as biodiversity conservation among farmers. Conventional crop protection strategies like fencing, patrolling, and scare techniques are labor-intensive, inconsistent and may not be viable in the long term. To eliminate these conflicts, this paper introduces an intelligent, AI-based system of real-time animal intrusion detection and automated deterrence. The proposed system uses the YOLO (You Only Look Once) deep learning model on robust and accurate detection of various animal species in different farm environments. A smart activation module will maximized energy consumption by keeping the vision system inactive when there is no movement occurring and activated when a possible motion is detected to cut down power usage and computational load. When this is successfully detected, the system records time-stamped photos, sends immediate messages to the farmers via a mobile app, and triggers non-invasive auditory signal to scare animals away safely. Moreover, environmental conditions like temperature and humidity are also constantly controlled to facilitate adaptive performance and to give farmers microclimate information to help him manage his crops better. Experimental analysis shows that the system has a low false-positive rate, high detection accuracy and latency of less than one second in reporting events, proving the appropriateness of the system in real-time application. The proposed system based on the combination of AI-based detection, automated alerting, and environmentally adjustable deterrence mechanisms provides a scalable and sustainable solution to improving the protection of crops and coexistence of humans and wildlife.
