TI  - Discovering Consumer Pathways Through UWB-Based Spatiotemporal Analytics in Electronics Stores for Smart Cities
AU  - J. Yan
AU  - C. Liu
AU  - Q. Zeng
AU  - S. Qiao
AU  - L. Liu
AU  - L. Cheng
T2  - IEEE Transactions on Consumer Electronics
AB  - As smart cities increasingly embed intelligence into commercial environments, data-driven analysis of consumer pathways in electronics stores has become crucial for understanding consumer decision-making, optimizing product layouts, and enhancing the shopping experience. However, existing consumer pathway analysis methods often suffer from limited data granularity and insufficient accuracy. To address these limitations, this paper proposes a novel process discovery algorithm that leverages Ultra-Wideband (UWB) positioning technology to capture consumer spatiotemporal data and discover consumer pathways in electronics retail environments. The proposed method systematically identifies high-frequency consumer trajectories and detects deviations between actual and expected behaviors, thereby more effectively supporting store layout optimization and enhancing the consumer experience. Experimental results from a real-world electronics store demonstrate that the proposed method significantly outperforms state-of-the-art process discovery techniques. On average, the consumer pathway discovered by the proposed method shows a 15% improvement in overall quality and a 24% increase in precision compared to those derived from existing techniques, validating the effectiveness and applicability of the approach. Furthermore, UWB-based process discovery can uncover potential correlations between pathway structures and purchasing decisions, offering actionable insights for smart retail operations within urban digital ecosystems.
