TI  - Enhancing Response Mechanisms Against LOLBAS Threats in SCADA Environments: Ablation Study and Practical Implications
AU  - T. Abu-Alzanat
AU  - J. A. Alzubi
AU  - A. AlDabbas
AU  - H. N. Fakhouri
AU  - H. Kanaker
AU  - O. A. Alzubi
T2  - 2026 2nd International Conference on Computational Intelligence Approaches and Applications (ICCIAA)
AB  - Living-off-the-land binaries and scripts (LOLBAS) enable adversaries to abuse trusted system utilities to perform malicious actions while minimizing the introduction of new executables. In supervisory control and data acquisition (SCADA) environments, this behavior is difficult to distinguish from legitimate administration and maintenance, and overly aggressive mitigation may jeopardize availability and safety. This paper presents a LOLBAS-aware detection and response framework that fuses host command telemetry with NetFlow-style network behavior. We extract lightweight semantic features from command lines using natural language processing and combine them with real-time flow summaries derived from packet capture. Detection is performed with a weighted ensemble of Random Forest, XGBoost, LSTM, and Transformer models, where vote weights are calibrated from validation F1-scores. To preserve operational safety, the response layer operates in alert-only mode, producing evidence-backed operator recommendations instead of automated containment. Experiments on 150,000 labeled records from a hardware-in-the-loop SCADA simulation and a 6–8 week continuous evaluation window achieve 95.2% accuracy, 2.8% false positive rate, 0.95 F1-score, 0.97 ROC-AUC, and 8.5 ms average inference latency. An ablation study shows that command semantics contribute the largest performance gain, while network context and temporal modeling further reduce false alarms.
