TY  - JOUR
TI  - Identification and Assessment of Timed Petri Nets
AU  - M. Montes-Partida
AU  - E. López-Mellado
T2  - IEEE Transactions on Automation Science and Engineering
AB  - This paper falls within the scope of discrete event process identification, where models are expressed using timed Petri nets (TPNs). The models are discovered from a set  $\lambda _{\boldsymbol {\tau }}$  of dated event sequences, and then the timing of the models are evaluated through a quality measure called accuracy. The paper presents an identification method that builds transition-TPN and a model evaluation method; the timing of transitions is expressed through two parameters: average  $\delta $ (t ${}_{\mathbf {j}}$ ) and interval  $\iota $ (t ${}_{\mathbf {j}}$ ) firing durations of transitions. The identification method operates in three stages. First, a PN  $N$  is built from the untimed sequences. Then, using the dated event sequences, the timing parameters of all the transitions of the TPN  ${N} _{\mathbf {T}}$  are determined. Afterwards, a structural and temporal refining technique is applied to  ${N} _{\mathbf {T}}$  to improve its accuracy. For each transition, the distribution of the computed firing delays is analysed; if there is more than one delay cluster, the transition is refined into two or more new transitions T’ according to the number of clusters. Later, the transitions in T’ are replaced in S, and their occurrence pattern is synthesised as a PN N’ using new places; then N’ is merged with N through T’ to obtain the refined TPN. On the other hand, two accuracy measures of TPN are proposed. A TPN  $N$  is assessed regarding  $\lambda _{\boldsymbol {\tau }}$ ; a metric is developed for each kind of timing  $\delta $ (t ${}_{\mathbf {j}}$ ) and  $\iota $ (t ${}_{\mathbf {j}}$ ). The accuracy of  $N$  is evaluated on [0,1] by measuring the deviations between the actual event occurrences in the traces in  $\lambda _{\boldsymbol {\tau }}$  and the expected firing instant of transitions in the TPN. Note to Practitioners—The method builds automatically a timed Petri net (TPN) model from the observed behaviour of a discrete-event process expressed as a set of dated event sequences  $\lambda _{\boldsymbol {\tau }}$ . Efficient algorithms allow building accurate models in which the time is associated with transitions to declare their firing durations. The timing is computed as an average  $\delta $ (tj) or an interval  $\iota $ (tj). Furthermore, two measures for assessing the quality of TPNs regarding each type of timing are presented. The measures evaluate the accuracy of the model based on the deviations between the actual firing of events in  $\lambda _{\tau }$  and the expected firing instant in the model.
