TY  - CONF
TI  - Integrated Edge-based Fault Analytics and Auto-Reclosing Control for Smart Three-Phase Distribution Systems
AU  - M. Muthukumaran
AU  - P. S
AU  - S. R
AU  - L. G
AU  - S. S
T2  - 2026 International Conference on AI-Driven Smart Systems and Ubiquitous Computing (ICAUC)
AB  - Traditional relays persist in holding back dependable fault management with their poor discrimination of momentary disturbances from permanent faults, lack of fine-grained phase-wise analysis, and failure to provide a uniform cloud-based visibility, especially in three-phase distribution systems. This gap creates the need for an edge-compatible, low-cost protection network architecture with real-time event-type classification and automated response mechanisms as well as remote monitoring. The designed scheme utilizes an ESP32 controller for the analysis of PT and CT readings with instant magnitude retrieval, while a constant threshold of 50 A is used to classify L-G, L-L, and three-phase faults. A persistence time of 0.05 s is used to eliminate transitory signals from sustained errors. For non-damageable faults, the schedule initiates a three-shot auto-reclosing operation, whilst for faults that do not clear, there is a transfer to lockout of appropriate quantities. MATLAB/Simulink simulation results show that all expected waveforms (voltage sag and inrush) at 0.04 s match the specified logic implemented in the program. Random hardware tests further validate consistent fault detection, transient fault restoration, and permanent fault lockout. Phase and fault recordings are transmitted to ThingSpeak every minute when a phase voltage is present, fault codes (0-3) or temporary/permanent event counts: see MATLAB analytics for verification of data availability. A practical defense and sensing layer for smart-grid/industrial/academic deployments, the system platform is also a scalable substrate for further penetration into higher voltage deployments, predictive maintenance, and more robust IoT communication stacks.
