TY  - CONF
TI  - Explainable Continual Learning for Edge IoT Surveillance with On-device Concept-Drift Detection
AU  - K. K
AU  - V. Parthipan
T2  - 2026 9th International Conference on Intelligent Computing and Control Systems (ICICCS)
AB  - The fast expansion of Internet of Things (IoT) implementations of smart surveillance has generated an immediate demand of adaptive, resource-efficient, and understandable edge learning systems. When subjected to concept drift, i.e. the underlying data distribution evolves as the environment, behaviors or threats change, traditional machine learning models deployed on IoT devices tend to reduce in performance. A problem with the current cloud-centric methods of addressing drift is that it is limited by latency, bandwidth, and privacy. In order to overcome these issues, the current study is a proposal of an Explainable Continual Learning (ECL) architecture of edge-based IoT surveillance systems, which is able to detect and adjust to concept drift directly in-situ. The suggested system combines lightweight continuous learning mechanisms with internal drift checking units, thus allowing the IoT devices to regularly update the models with the old information stored. Moreover, explainability mechanisms make sure that there are transparency in decision-making so that stakeholders can know and embrace the adaptive behavior of the system in a real time. Benchmark experimental appraisals on IoT surveillance datasets have revealed that the ECL framework has enhanced adaptability, less communication overhead, and increased interpretability than the current methods. This paper aids in growing reliable and sustainable AI at the edge, accelerate IoT surveillance systems, and humanize them.
