AU  - W. Zhou
T2  - IGARSS 2026 - 2026 IEEE International Geoscience and Remote Sensing Symposium
AB  - The Clustering Vector Quantized Variational Autoencoder (CVQ-VAE) has gained prominence in analyzing complex well log data, owing to its isomorphic alignment with the discrete nature of lithological stratification. However, conventional evaluation paradigms heavily reliant on reconstruction metrics, such as Mean Squared Error (MSE), are insufficient for assessing the structural integrity of the quantization codebook. High reconstruction fidelity often masks "codebook collapse," a phenomenon where the model overfits utilizing a constrained vocabulary, thereby severely undermining generalization and physical interpretability. To mitigate this interpretability crisis, this paper introduces a multi-dimensional evaluation framework specifically tailored for geophysical logging. By synthesizing intrinsic codebook metrics with latent space visualization techniques, our approach rigorously audits the discriminative power of the learned representations. Experiments conducted on a large-scale dataset from the Ordos Basin demonstrate that this framework not only diagnoses latent redundancy but also quantitatively determines the optimal embedding complexity for diverse geological curves. By verifying that reconstruction fidelity stems from a robust, well-structured codebook rather than superficial memorization, our methodology guarantees the reliability of deep learning models in quantitative geophysical interpretation.
