AB  - Non-Technical Losses (NTLs) from theft, metering errors, and billing irregularities continue to affect distribution utilities by reducing revenue, distorting load measurements, and complicating regulatory compliance. In many jurisdictions, the associated financial impact reaches billions of dollars annually. The deployment of Advanced Metering Infrastructure (AMI) and improved data acquisition systems has expanded the analytical options for electricity theft detection (ETD), leading to techniques ranging from statistical screening and machine learning to deep representation learning, state-estimation-based inference, and privacy-preserving computation. Evaluation is increasingly shaped by deployment factors such as computational cost, privacy exposure, inspection overhead, and regulatory requirements. This review provides a structured overview of current ETD methods and highlights emerging research directions, including federated analytics, DER-aware modelling, adversarial behavior, and zerotrust metering.
