TI  - HDLF-CUMS: A Context-Aware Hybrid Deep Learning Framework for Proactive Threat Detection in University Digital Ecosystems
AU  - V. T. Nguyen
AU  - T. A. Nguyen
AU  - V. T. Cao
AU  - V. H. Vu
AU  - V. C. Bui
T2  - 2025 2nd Beyond Technology Summit on Informatics International Conference (BTS-I2C)
AB  - The digital transformation of higher education institutions has culminated in complex Comprehensive University Management Systems (CUMS), which, while enhancing operational efficiency, have significantly expanded the cyber-attack surface. Existing security paradigms, predicated upon predefined signatures and static rule-sets, are demonstrably inadequate against sophisticated application-layer threats and context-specific anomalies endemic to these environments. This paper introduces HDLF- CUMS, a novel, context-aware hybrid deep learning framework engineered for proactive threat detection within these unique digital ecosystems. The proposed architecture synergistically integrates three discrete yet complementary analytical modules operating on a unified, normalized log data lake: (1) a Deep Neural Network (DNN) for high-confidence classification of known attack patterns; (2) a Variational Autoencoder (VAE) for unsupervised discovery of novel, zero-day anomalies by modeling the system’s normative operational behavior distribution; and (3) a Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) network for the sequential analysis of user and process behaviors to identify anomalous temporal correlations. To validate the framework’s robustness and efficacy, the construction of VNU-IS-Log, a high-fidelity 5GB+ labeled dataset derived from a real-world CUMS, is detailed. This dataset is uniquely enriched with emulated attack scenarios that replicate previously documented, real-world security incidents. A rigorous empirical evaluation demonstrates that HDLF-CUMS is projected to achieve an accuracy exceeding 95.8% and an F1-score of 96.2%, thereby significantly outperforming traditional machine learning classifiers and standalone deep learning models. Crucially, the framework is designed to reduce the Mean Time To Detect (MTTD) for critical application-layer attacks to under five seconds, offering a quantifiable and viable pathway towards achieving a state of cyber resilience in higher education infrastructures.
