TI  - An Adaptive Defensive Mechanism for DQN Storage Resources Allocation from Advanced Persistent Threats
AU  - U. Sakthivelu
AU  - C. N. S. Vinoth Kumar
T2  - 2023 International Conference on Innovative Computing, Intelligent Communication and Smart Electrical Systems (ICSES)
AB  - Attackers that utilize Advanced Persistent Threats (APTs) use a wide array of sophisticated methods to steal information from firms that are the targets of their attacks in a way that is both ongoing and undetected. They may even coerce the storage system into using a certain protective strategy, only to be attacked in retaliation for their actions. For the purpose of illustrating the exchanges that take place between anAPT attacker and a defensive party, this research study utilized a model based on the Colonel Blotto game. The defensive side uses a well devised strategy to distribute their central processing units (CPUs) across the various storage devices that are contained within an organizational system. The purpose of this study is to investigate the Nashilibria (NEs) that are associated with the distribution of central processing units (CPUs) in settings that include both symmetric and asymmetric CPUs. The interaction between an APT attacker and a defensive is the primary focus of the inquiry. The purpose of this research is to investigate the impact that restricted CPU resources, data storage capacity, and the number of storage devices will have on the level of data security that is expected to be maintained by the organization's storage system as well as the efficiency of that system. The purpose of this study is to investigate the relationships between a limited amount of CPU resources, a restricted amount of data storage capacity, and the number of storage devices. This research suggests a method for allocating CPU resources that blends policy hill-climbing (PHC) with the Greedy technique. Even without prior knowledge of the APT attack model or the data storage model, the goal is to improve the effectiveness of the defender's APT defense in a dynamic game situation. This is the case even though the knowledge gap exists. This strategy draws on previous experiences gained when navigating similar situations in order to rapidly establish quality standards and accelerate the learning process. The usage of a Q-network (DQN) for the allocation of central processing units (CPUs) provides a considerable improvement in the detection performance APTs in scenarios that comprise a significant quantity of CPUs. In light of the findings of the simulation, it has been hypothesized that the usage of reinforcement learning for CPU allocation, as opposed to Q learning, has the potential to increase the degree of data security.
