AB  - Protective relay communication networks form the backbone of modern protection engineering by ensuring the safe, reliable, and efficient operation of electrical power systems. As power grids become more complex and interconnected, the role of relay networks extends beyond fault detection and interruption to encompass system stability, selective coordination, and cybersecurity resilience. These networks provide the critical first line of defense against equipment damage, cascading outages, and safety hazards to personnel, minimizing the impact of electrical faults through rapid and precise action. Their importance lies not only in the individual protective devices themselves, but in the communication and coordination between relays, which enable adaptive responses to evolving grid and/or power distribution conditions. A robust relay network integrates redundancy, intelligence, and interoperability, offering engineers the tools to safeguard infrastructure while supporting the growing power demands of data centers, renewable integration, and digital monitoring. In this context, protective relay networks serve as both guardians of reliability and enablers of innovation, underscoring their indispensable role in the discipline of protective engineering.
