AU  - S. Jiyani
T2  - 2026 International Conference on Natural Language Processing and Computer Vision (ICNPCV)
AB  - Cloud-based enterprise systems produce enormous amounts of security telemetry data from identities, workloads, networks, storage services and administrative actions. In Microsoft Azure, this information can be found in several services, including Azure Activity Logs, Microsoft Entra ID logs, Microsoft Defender for Cloud, Azure Monitor Logs, Key Vault logs, Storage logs, Network Security Group flow logs, and Microsoft Sentinel. However, traditional detection systems that rely on defined signatures and thresholds are ineffective in detecting complex and multi-stage and behavior-driven attacks. This research outlines an artificial intelligence based security analytics and remediation system for Microsoft Azure environment that provides automated remediation. The proposed architecture includes Azure-native telemetry sources connected to a centralized security data pipeline, a preprocessing and feature extraction pipeline with Kusto Query Language, and a hybrid AI classifier model, which involves supervised learning, anomaly detection and contextual risk scoring capabilities, to classify threats as low, medium, high or critical. This proposed framework was tested using a synthetic dataset of Azure security telemetry of 1,250,000 log events each, comprising 1,231,260 legitimate events and 18,740 malicious or suspicious events. The proposed AI model outperformed the traditional rule-based model for all major metrics related to the threat detection. It also cut down on the false-positive rate, making it 8.7% down to 3.2% as well as decrease the mean time to detect, from 14.2 minutes, to 4.8 minutes with automation and the mean time to respond, from 31.5 minutes, to 7.4 minutes with automation. With the help of these results, combining predictive analytics with playbooks and Defender-based response actions in Azure Sentinel will enhance the capabilities of threat detection, reduce alert fatigue, and empower faster response efforts to the cloud environment.
