TI  - Critical Infrastructure Security Using Computer Vision Technologies
AU  - Xindi Zhang
AU  - Ebroul Izquierdo
AU  - Krishna Chandramouli
T2  - Security Technologies and Social Implications
AB  - Modern critical infrastructures (CI) are increasingly turning into distributed, complex Cyber&#x2010;physical systems that need proactive protection and fast restoration to mitigate physical or cyber incidents or attacks. While the challenge of cyber threats has been well addressed in the literature with mitigation strategies ranging from organizational policies, technology measures to detect attacks such as Ransonware, Wannacry, the scope of physical security of modern CI is not reported in the literature exhaustively. The recent developments in Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs) has provided a new&#x2010;age weapon to be used against CI. Similarly, the lack of surveillance available across critical infrastructure installations also offers an opportunity to cause disruptions to the service delivery of CI. Addressing the need to enhance and improve the quality of surveillance and physical security against intruders (both UAVs and unauthorized personnel), the paper presents the use of advances in the field of computer vision systems for effectively mitigating against such attacks. The visual analytics framework presented in the paper encompasses a holistic framework that brings together key scientific innovations in the field of object detection using Region&#x2010;based Fully Connected Network (RFCN) deep&#x2010;learning architecture to mitigate against the physical threat. The paper presents a framework that integrates four main computer vision technologies, namely (i) UAV detector; (ii) person detection; (iii) person reidentification; and (iv) face recognition to enhance the operational security of critical infrastructure perimeter. The novelty of the proposed framework relies on the integration of key technical innovations that satisfies the operational requirements of critical infrastructure in using computer vision technologies. The various components of the proposed framework have been validated using commercially available graphical processing units for detecting intruders. The performance of the proposed framework has been evaluated in operational environments of the critical infrastructure.
