TI  - Real-Time Cryptojacking Threat Mitigation Using Anomaly and Behavioral Analysis
AU  - N. R. S. Kumar
AU  - N. L. Reddy
AU  - D. M. Naidu
AU  - A. C. K. Naidu
AU  - J. J. P. Reddy
AU  - V. Kumar
T2  - 2026 International Conference on Wireless Communications Signal Processing and Networking (WiSPNET)
AB  - Cryptojacking has emerged as a significant cybersecurity threat, where attackers exploit system resources for unauthorized cryptocurrency mining. Traditional signature-based detection systems often fail to detect modern stealthy mining malware. This paper proposes a hybrid real-time detection framework integrating anomaly detection and behavioral analysis to identify host-based cryptojacking attacks. A balanced dataset of 40,000 executable samples (20,000 benign and 20,000 cryptojacking malware samples) was constructed using verified malware repositories and controlled sandbox environments. Static features (API calls, PE headers, cryptographic libraries) and dynamic features (CPU usage, memory consumption, I/O operations, network activity) were extracted and processed. Supervised models including Random Forest, Support Vector Machine, and Convolutional Neural Networks were implemented alongside an unsupervised Autoencoder model. The proposed hybrid model achieved 98.4% accuracy, 97.9% precision, 98.1% recall, and $98.0 \% ~\mathrm{F} 1$ - score with an AUC of 0.99. The system also includes automated mitigation mechanisms for terminating malicious processes.
