TI  - A Practical Fog-Based Deep Vision System for Wildlife Intrusion Monitoring
AU  - M. Sachan
AU  - S. V
T2  - 2026 International Conference on Computing, Communication, Control and Cyber-Physical Systems (I5CPS)
AB  - Farmers in forest regions often struggle with wild animals entering their fields. This problem continues to disrupt agricultural activity and cause financial loss. Traditional monitoring systems tend to be slow or unreliable, particularly in places where Internet connectivity is often fluctuating. To address these limitations, this study explores fog-based intrusion detection setup that combines local IoT sensors with a lightweight deep-learning model. Instead of sending every frame to a remote server, the system handles most of the computation on a device located near to the farmland. During preliminary trials, YOLOv5s was compared with the two-stage Faster R-CNN model to understand their suitability for real-time use on a fog node. YOLOv5 delivered better results, making it a more practical choice for deployment. Using these findings, an architecture is shaped in which the fog device performs the detection tasks, while the cloud stores event logs and provides access to past intrusion records. Although some components of the full system are still being implemented, the benchmarking study and design analysis done so far indicates that a fog-based setup can improve responsiveness despite weak network conditions.
