AU  - U. T. Nguyen
T2  - 2025 2nd International Generative AI and Computational Language Modelling Conference (GACLM)
AB  - One of the challenges in developing machine learning models for money laundering detection is the severe class imbalance in anti-money laundering (AML) datasets. To mitigate class imbalance, oversampling methods such as SMOTE and ADASYN are commonly used to create additional samples for the minority class. However, these methods operate on flat feature representations and fail to capture graph-based structures that are characteristic of money laundering behaviors. In this paper, we propose a model based on Conditional Generative Adversarial Networks (CGAN) to generate synthetic money laundering graph patterns that preserve both transaction attributes and graph connectivity of underlying AML data. Experimental results on multiple datasets show that the proposed CGAN model generates synthetic data that enable money laundering detection models to achieve significantly higher predictive performance in terms of recall and false alert rates, compared to SMOTE and ADASYN.
