AB  - This research aims to develop a novel framework for the early detection of unauthorized access by leveraging multimodal data fusion techniques using Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) and Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks. The system is designed to analyze both logs data from threats surveillance streams and behavioral event logs, enabling it to capture anomalies and temporal anomalies with high precision. Specifically, the first approach processes 30 logs samples through CNNs to extract features related to access patterns, while the second approach utilizes 45 logs samples analyzed using a CNN-LSTM hybrid model to understand contextual and sequential patterns. The proposed framework achieves a high operational accuracy of 93%, with strong sensitivity and specificity in detecting unusual access behavior, and results that are statistically significant ($\mathbf{p}<0.05$). By improving upon conventional CNN-only methods, the hybrid architecture enhances the real-time responsiveness of the system, contributing significantly to the advancement of intelligent cybersecurity systems capable of proactive threat detection and alerting.
