TY  - CHAP
TI  - Synchrophasor Data Anomaly Detection for Wide‐Area Monitoring and Control in Cyber‐Power Systems
AU  - A.K. Srivastava
AU  - S. Pandey
AU  - A. Ahmed
AU  - S. Basumalik
AU  - S.K. Sadanandan
T2  - Smart Cyber-Physical Power Systems, Volume 1: Fundamental Concepts, Challenges, and Solutions
AB  - Summary <p>The power grid situational awareness relies on real&#x2010;time measurement obtained from sensors like phasor measurement units (PMUs), remote terminal units, and intelligent electronic devices. With its fast reporting and GPS, synchronized measurements can provide a wide area view of the system that can be vital to operators for enhanced decision&#x2010;making capabilities. Applications based on these measurements help control room operators take corrective actions for reliable and resilient power grid operation. However, these measurements' latency and data quality may result in inaccurate output from the applications, resulting in wrong control affecting the power grid operation. Anomalies may include any deviation from expected measurements resulting from power system events or bad data. Bad data include data errors or loss of information due to malfunctions in supporting synchrophasor cyber infrastructure. It is necessary to flag/remove or correct any bad data before utilizing it for an application.</p> <p>This chapter first discusses the synchrophasor data flow and causes of data anomalies, data characteristics, and types such as time series concept drift with grid dynamics, comparing different machine learning techniques based on the data type. Next, the chapter provides the effects of data anomalies on applications that rely on synchrophasor data. Finally, the chapter will present real&#x2010;time data anomalies detection methods in synchrophasor measurements and their classification into bad and event data. Synchrophasor anomaly detection and classification (SyADC) tool will be discussed using an ensemble technique. An unsupervised ML, which combines fast and accurate outlier detection algorithms such as isolation forest and local outlier probability, is used as base detectors in SyADC. The chapter also discusses &#x201c;Quality&#x2010;aware&#x201d; synchrophasor&#x2010;based monitoring and control applications and performance under different data anomalies and improved performance when the applications were made quality&#x2010;aware. Simulation results for developed algorithms will be provided with satisfactory results for the IEEE test system and PMU data from the western US interconnection (WECC).</p>
