TY  - CONF
TI  - A Hybrid CNN-LSTM-KDE Framework for Interval Prediction of Slurry Density
AU  - L. Wang
AU  - Z. Liu
AU  - W. Dai
AU  - J. Nan
AU  - Z. Xia
AU  - H. Wang
T2  - 2026 Joint International Conference on Automation-Intelligence-Safety (ICAIS) & International Symposium on Autonomous Systems (ISAS)
AB  - High-precision and real-time detection of slurry density is a prerequisite for optimizing mineral processing control and guaranteeing concentrate grade and recovery efficiency. Targeting the limitations of traditional detection methods—specifically severe hysteresis and inadequate precision—this study presents an intelligent interval prediction framework for slurry density integrating Convolutional Neural Networks (CNN), Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks, and Kernel Density Estimation (KDE). The proposed method establishes a hybrid CNN-LSTM architecture. Specifically, a CNN leverages local perception and weight-sharing mechanisms to adaptively extract spatial features from time-series data while mitigating high-frequency noise. Simultaneously, the LSTM network exploits its gating mechanism to capture long-term temporal dependencies, facilitating high-precision deterministic point prediction. To quantify uncertainty, KDE is subsequently applied to model the probability density of prediction residuals, generating confidence intervals that reliably bracket the ground truth. Validation using industrial data from a large-scale mineral processing plant indicates superior performance compared to state-of-the-art baselines. The proposed model achieved an MAE of 6.89 kg/m3, an RMSE of 8.66 kg/m3, and an R2 of 99.56%. Moreover, it demonstrated excellent reliability with a PICP of 0.9492 and a PINAW of 0.0752, highlighting its exceptional accuracy and robustness in complex industrial environments.
