TY  - CONF
TI  - Integration of Reinforcement Agents and Self-Supervised Representation Learning for Real-Time Cyberthreat Analysis: Direction of Cyber Defense Intelligent
AU  - N. H. S
AU  - S. K
T2  - 2025 2nd International Conference on Artificial Intelligence and Knowledge Discovery in Concurrent Engineering (ICECONF)
AB  - Cyber threats are falling into the category of ‘cooler and more advanced’ meaning that intelligent cyber security systems that predict crime are on the horizon. Reactive security (such as traditional anti-virus solutions) is hardly capable of defending against zero-day vulnerabilities and APTs, which make use of more advanced exploitation techniques, and are not detectable with traditional signature-based, static-rule-based security mechanisms. In this paper, we propose a proactive cyber threat prediction model based on the RL and SSL, to identify the predicting the cyber threat in real time with high realization and prediction accuracy. Our framework is a two-stage pipeline. In the first phase, SSL is applied to pre-train representations from the unlabeled log data, network traffic and endpoint telemetry to discover latent threat signals with no human supervision. At the second stage, RL agents are implemented, working on real-time data, and based on simulated environments, which will be learning to classify possible threats on a fly and then act on them by finding an optimal policies. RL reward function: it further tunes the RL reward function to adapt to new attack patterns that the agents evolve. Model assessment was conducted on multiple standard cybersecurity datasets (CICIDS2017, UNSW-NB15, and inhouse attack simulations). Our results showed the superior performance of the SCAEI framework in terms of early detection accuracy (greater than 94%), the reduction of the false positive rate, and the faster adaptation to the new attack vectors, as compared to the traditional supervised models. Second, the system also generalizes very well to entirely unseen threats, since it relies on SSL-assisted feature learning. In this way, we address gaps in computer security needs by moving from reactive approach to predictive one by employing an innovative blend of sophisticated machine learning techniques that are able to learn the concealed behavior without assuming any labels or pre-defined heuristics. The paper shows a scalable and modular framework that must be deployable in real-time cloud-based data centres or serverless edge computing zones. To this end, the work integrates the behavior-policy-based adaptive decision-making algorithm of RL with unsupervised representational transferring knowledge from SSL and building a strong defense against adaptive cyber adversaries.
