AB  - Aiming at the problem that the prediction of job runtime in high-performance computing systems ignores temporal and semantic features, this paper proposes a job runtime prediction method named TSRunPred, aiming to improve the prediction accuracy of job runtime in high-performance computing systems. This method innovatively integrates temporal and semantic features. It captures long-term dependencies and short-term fluctuations in time series data through the Long Short-Term Memory Network and Convolutional Neural Network. Meanwhile, it extracts semantic information from job submission paths and job names with the help of the DistilBERT model. The model was verified on 52,670 job log data. The results show that TS-RunPred outperforms comparative models such as Support Vector Regression, Multilayer Perceptron, Decision Tree, and XgBoost in terms of indicators such as Mean Absolute Error, Root Mean Squared Error, and Coefficient of Determination. Compared with XgBoost, the MAE of TS-RunPred decreased by 59.1%, the RMSE decreased by 46.9%, and the $R^{2}$ increased by 8.6%. This indicates that TSRunPred can significantly improve the accuracy of job runtime prediction and provide more reliable decision support for job scheduling in HPC systems.
