AU  - R. Li
T2  - 2025 4th International Conference on Power System and Power Engineering (PSPE)
AB  - This paper presents an efficient solution for power equipment monitoring and fault prediction tasks, combining large model fine-tuning and edge deployment technologies to enhance the intelligence level of power systems. Based on the BERT model and incorporating few-shot learning and transfer learning strategies, fine-tuning on power equipment log data significantly improves fault prediction accuracy. Incremental learning and Elastic Weight Consolidation (EWC) techniques are employed to address the catastrophic forgetting problem in long-term device operation. To accommodate the resource limitations of edge devices, knowledge distillation, neural network pruning, and quantization techniques are used to optimize the model, ensuring efficient deployment and real-time inference on resource-constrained devices. Experimental results show that the optimized BERT model outperforms traditional methods in power equipment fault detection and load prediction tasks, both in terms of accuracy and inference speed. Furthermore, testing on different hardware platforms validates the superiority of the edge deployment solution in terms of inference speed and memory consumption. This paper provides an effective technical path for intelligent monitoring and fault prediction of power equipment.
