TI  - DGTLMD: Unsupervised Lateral Movement Detection via Dynamic Graph Transformer
AU  - Y. Du
AU  - J. Feng
AU  - Y. Li
AU  - Z. Liu
AU  - L. Zhang
AU  - Y. Wen
T2  - 2026 29th International Conference on Computer Supported Cooperative Work in Design (CSCWD)
AB  - Lateral Movement (LM) in Advanced Persistent Threat (APT) attacks constitutes a critical phase for attackers to achieve deep penetration, posing a significant threat to network security. Although recent studies have attempted to adopt dynamic graph learning-based methods for LM detection, these approaches generally suffer from limitations such as insufficient modeling of temporal dynamics, poor scalability, over-smoothing and over-compression issues in deep Graph Neural Networks (GNNs), and misalignment between training objectives and anomaly detection tasks. To address these limitations, we propose DGTLMD, an unsupervised detection framework that innovatively integrates dynamic graph sequences with the Transformer architecture. DGTLMD first constructs host-centric dynamic interaction subgraphs from log data and introduces a novel time series tokenization scheme that incorporates structural topology, edge attributes, and fine-grained temporal dynamics. Subsequently, the Transformer model is leveraged to perform autoregressive modeling on the generated token sequences, effectively capturing long-range behavioral dependencies while avoiding the over-smoothing and over-squashing issues inherent in traditional GNN-based approaches. During the detection phase, a sliding-window mechanism is employed for real-time anomaly detection. We conducted experimental evaluations of DGTLMD on two large-scale datasets LANL and OpTC, and the results demonstrate its excellent performance in LM detection.
C3  - 2026 29th International Conference on Computer Supported Cooperative Work in Design (CSCWD)
