AB  - These days, it appears to be possible to automate workflows for the purpose of optimizing industrial processes using digital twins. While Industry 4.0's enabling technologies have reached a mature state, manufacturing process capabilities like process control and monitoring, quality evaluation, and process modelling can be addressed with the abundance of sensors as well as information processing techniques available. Nevertheless, a number of potential approaches, such metamodeling languages, and technologies that come after computer-integrated manufacturing have not been sufficiently utilised. This is achieved by introducing a framework that utilizes a computerized workflow knowledge database, a technology classification, and a metamodeling language. Developing digital twins for manufacturing process design and operation while including humans will be made much easier with this method. In the future, investigators will be able to discover resource bottlenecks, poor process execution, lost time, and idle time and convey these findings to process owners with the aid of digital procedure maps and simulations. Several situations can then be chosen, modelled, and put into practice based on this. Digital operation twins ultimately improve a company's overall effectiveness and greatly aid in digital transition by optimizing both physical objects and processes themselves.
