TI  - Investigating Stacked Ensemble Model for Oil Reservoir Characterisation
AU  - P. Saikia
AU  - R. D. Baruah
T2  - 2019 IEEE International Conference on Systems, Man and Cybernetics (SMC)
AB  - Ensembling of machine learning models is quite popular in the domain of predictive modelling. When used with proper diversity of algorithms, the ensemble model provides significant result compared to its associated base models. We proposed here a Stacked Ensemble model, named as StackNet for Oil Reservoir Characterisation, that can be assumed as a feedforward neural network with only a forward propagation that enables us to decide the network architecture during runtime. It is the model obtained by stacking layers of ensemble models one above the other successively until we reach a desired stopping criteria. Oil Reservoir Characterisation, that helps geo-scientist to understand a reservoir for the presence of oil, has always been a challenging problem due to the heterogeneous nature of earth subsurface and the complex relationship of the geological and geophysical data involved. The proposed model in the field of reservoir characterisation integrated with the domain understanding can provide a better generalised model for the prediction of reservoir properties of even a complex reservoir. K fold cross validation paradigm with each fold corresponds to data from a separate oil well is used to make the model capable to make robust decision with the reservoir data that varies from place to place. To validate our model, we have performed various experiments on a reservoir dataset constituting seismic attributes as input and well log data as output. The experiments demonstrate how the approach improves the generalisation capability of different individual models involved, and seems to be a promising approach for reservoir characterisation for predicting the complex reservoir properties that varies over the reservoir.
