TI  - An Explainable Hybrid Self-Attention–Enhanced Deep Neural Network for Malware Detection
AU  - S. Jophin
AU  - R. Subramanian R
T2  - 2026 International Conference on Integrated Intelligence and Cognitive Engineering (ICIICE)
AB  - As malware attacks on Linux systems grow more advanced, the use of more complex signatures to detect them usually fails when it comes to detecting new threats. Detection approaches, which examines runtime process activity, has become a useful alternative, and recognizes dynamic patterns of malicious behavior. The proposed study presents an effective and explainable ai based deep learning architecture Self-Attention-Enhanced Deep Neural Network (XAI-S-AEDNN) to detect malware using behavior in Linux systems. A dataset of 100,000 samples of Linux process execution containing 35 system-level features such as CPU usage, memory consumption, scheduling parameters, page faults, and metrics of context switches were obtained in order to sample both benign and malicious activity. It is preprocessed with median imputation, Z-score normalization, one-hot encoding, and removal of outliers and then hybrid feature engineering is performed, extracting temporal, statistical, and interaction-based features. The feature set is then reduced to 25 most discriminating features by a hybrid selection mechanism. These fined features are fed to a S-AEDNN of combining dense embeddings, self-attention and deep fully connected layers trained with Binary Cross-Entropy loss and Adam optimizer. The explainable AI methods such as attention visualization, SHAP, and LIME give both global and local interpretability of predictions. Experimental results indicate that S-AEDNN has a 99.21% accuracy, 99.36% recall and 99.74% of AUC-ROC which is better than the baseline models and indicates the potential of the S-AEDNN as a useful and transparent Linux malware detector operating in real-time and in an operational cyber security environment.
