AU  - S. Gottipati
T2  - IEEE Pervasive Computing
AB  - Maintenance error is a major cause of accidents in critical systems, underscoring the need to strengthen maintenance resiliency. Persistent gaps include fragmented documentation, stagnant skills training, and tacit knowledge loss, all of which clash with the embodied, sensor-rich realities of frontline maintenance work. This review identifies stress factors, namely, skill decay for rare tasks, procedural deviations, and cognitive overload, and addresses these gaps through a socio-technical lens and explores embodied pervasive maintenance solutions. The proposed three paradigm shifts reimagine the maintenance ecosystem: low-fidelity VR trainers that preserve task-critical affordances and cues while reducing realism cost; AI-powered semantic quality assurance pipelines to standardize maintenance procedures; and learning cognitive assistants to provide technicians just-in-time guidance while capturing field heuristics. Together, the paradigm shifts reimagine maintenance as a ubiquitous process that strengthens maintenance resiliency amid accelerating technology and obsolescence cycles.
