TY  - CONF
TI  - Defocus Degree Estimation for Event Camera with Two-Stage Contrast Maximization
AU  - Z. Wang
AU  - D. Kong
T2  - 2025 International Conference on Signal Processing, Computer Networks and Communications (SPCNC)
AB  - As a common visual degradation process, defocus blur presents unique complexities in the imaging of event cameras. Compared with the defocus of images from ordinary cameras, the processing of defocus blur in event cameras is more challenging. This is caused by the sparse and asynchronous characteristics of event data in space and time. Event cameras lack the pixel information of traditional cameras, so conventional defocus restoration methods for regular images cannot be directly applied. Since judging the defocus degree of defocused event data plays a decisive role in the autofocus of event cameras, we sought effective optimization algorithms based on event images. First, the optical flow method was used to eliminate motion defocus from the sample data, compensating for image blur caused by motion by calculating the motion vectors of pixel points. Second, an energy function based on a physical model was constructed to simulate the blurring process of clear images. By optimizing the focusing params in the function, and using the mean squared error (MSE), structural similarity index (SSIM), and peak signal-to-noise ratio (PSNR) as optimization objectives, the best match between the blurred clear image and the actual defocused image was achieved. Finally, an accurate assessment of image defocus was realized.
