TY  - CONF
TI  - Predicting Machine Failures in Industrial Systems: A Comparative Analysis of AutoML-Based Multiclass Classification Models
AU  - A. S. Lokman
AU  - U. K. Jamaludin
AU  - O. J. Sheng
T2  - 2025 IEEE 9th International Conference on Software Engineering & Computer Systems (ICSECS)
AB  - In industrial systems, machine failures can result in costly downtime and operational inefficiencies. Predicting these failures in advance is crucial for minimizing disruptions and optimizing maintenance strategies. This paper presents a comparative analysis of AutoML-based multiclass classification models for predicting machine failures using historical log data. An end-to-end machine learning pipeline is designed and implemented, processing raw machine log entries into structured, time-aware features suitable for predictive analysis. The forecasting task is formulated as a multiclass classification problem, including a “no error” class to account for normal operations. Using ML.NET AutoML, multiple machine learning algorithms such as FastTree, FastForest, and LightGBM are automatically selected, trained, and tuned. Models are evaluated based on micro-accuracy, macroaccuracy, log loss, and log loss reduction to comprehensively assess performance. The results demonstrate the effectiveness of AutoML in automating model development and optimizing predictive accuracy, while highlighting important trade-offs between different evaluation metrics. This study offers practical insights and guidelines for implementing predictive maintenance systems in industrial environments using AutoML-based approaches.
