TI  - Transformer-Based for Sonic Log Prediction Using Conventional Well Log Measurement
AU  - A. Rahman
AU  - F. L. Gaol
AU  - H. Soeparno
AU  - Y. Arifin
T2  - 2025 International Conference on ICT for Smart Society (ICISS)
AB  - Sonic log data are crucial for subsurface analysis. This is due to their necessity for seismic-to-well tied calibration, lithology identification, or porosity estimate. The issue is that sonic data are infrequently accessible due to several factors, including the limited availability of sonic equipment within specific logging suites, suboptimal well conditions, high costs, exclusion from the initial logging plan, or the restriction of sonic logging to particular zones. This paper presents a method for generating synthetic sonic data by artificial intelligence, specifically utilizing the Transformers architecture, by leveraging existing logging data, including GR, RILD, RILM, SP, RHOB, RHOC, CNPOR, PE, and DCAL from the JARBOE 21 well in Kansas. The transformers effectively caught the correlation and dependence of multivariate depth and subsurface log data, providing remarkable experimental results: RMSE of $\mathbf{1. 9 3 ~} \boldsymbol{\mu} \mathbf{s} / \mathbf{f t}$, MAE of 1.398, and $\mathbf{R}^{\mathbf{2}}$ of 0.984 on the test dataset. These findings possess significant potential for application in wells without sonic log data, or for rectifying and supplementing absent or inaccurate sonic data without necessitating the logging procedure in the well.
