TI  - AI-Native Observability and Self-Evolving Diagnostics: Generative Troubleshooting, Semantic Correlation, and Embedded Guidance for Large-Scale Cloud Systems
AU  - S. B. Paramasivam Murugesan
AU  - D. Ganesan
T2  - 2026 6th International Conference on Image Processing and Capsule Networks (ICIPCN)
AB  - Modern microservices architectures are too complex for traditional threshold-based monitoring, necessitating a shift toward AI-native observability. This paper presents a new methodology for generative artificial intelligence based self-evolving diagnostics that go well beyond passive alerting. By combining semantic correlation engines with generative troubleshooting models, the system reasons about the motivations of system anomalies rather than simply indicating metric deviations. The study uses a unique data set of 416 separate cyber event logs, generated from a simulated hyperscale cloud environment. The proposed observability pipeline is validated through an experimental validation implemented with a custom-built software package written in Python and using the TensorFlow library for DL applications and the Scikit-learn for clustering. The key process is a two-tier neural model that maps unstructured log data over structured metric streams into human-interpretable action recommendations. The proposed system reduces mean time to resolution by 59% and achieves a strong success rate in predicting cascading failures before they affect service availability. The results demonstrate that embedding generative guidance within the observability stack, a.k.a. explanatory reporting, enables the system to evolve its own diagnostic capabilities by learning from prior resolutions, allowing it to handle novel failures without manual rule setting or reconfiguration.
