TY  - CONF
TI  - Enhancing Residential Gas Safety through Remote Monitoring and Embedded Microchip Systems
AU  - J. Liu
AU  - X. Liu
AU  - C. Jiang
T2  - 2025 7th International Conference on Internet of Things, Automation and Artificial Intelligence (IoTAAI)
AB  - Residential use of combustible gases (for example, LPG or natural gas) creates several safety issues, as missed leaks can result in fires, explosions, and harm or death. Relying on simple conventional detectors or inspecting manually is usually insufficient to protect a site. The paper proposes an entirely innovative gas safety system that integrates sensing with embedded microcontrollers, wireless monitoring, and automated actuation enhanced by artificial intelligence (AI) at the edge. The system combines low-cost semiconductor gas sensors such as MQ-series gas sensors with an Arduino or NodeMCU microchip to regularly measure the gas concentration. A lightweight machine learning model runs locally on the device, enabling accurate anomaly detection while reducing false positives caused by environmental fluctuations. When levels exceed a safe threshold, the system sounds a local alarm, activates a shut-off valve, and immediately transmits an alert to remote users via IoT (for example, Wi-Fi/cloud and SMS) for rapid intervention. The system alarms locally when gas concentration levels reach dangerous levels, operates a shut-off valve, and alarms remotely via Wi-Fi or GSM to enable user action within a reasonable time frame. The cloud maintains a digital twist of the system, which provides the system with real-time synchronization, predictive maintenance, and historical analytics. The communication is strong due to multi-network support, such as Wi-Fi, GSM, and optional LoRaWAN. The security protocols, including encrypted MQTT protocols and blockchain anchoring of event logs, provide traceability and data integrity. The paper also examines prevailing gas-monitoring technologies and shows our prototype’s design approach, outlining the sensors, microcontroller, and communication used. The suggested architecture (shown in Fig. 1) uses local processing and a connection to the cloud to ensure round-the-clock monitoring. We examine how different sensors perform and what problems they may have, and we offer helpful advice for their future development. The proposed solution is based on AI and the IoT, and it aims to be scalable, power-efficient, and smart-home-integrated to improve the safety of residents and industries using gas with a practical and future-oriented solution.
