TY  - CONF
TI  - Intelligent Access Control Mechanism Using Explainable AI for Cloud-Enabled E-Healthcare Environments
AU  - M. Sindhu
AU  - K. A. Davamani
T2  - 2026 13th International Conference on Computing for Sustainable Global Development (INDIACom)
AB  - With the increasing digitalization of the healthcare environment using cloud power, an important challenge is provision of secure, transparent and context-aware access to sensitive patient data. Access control techniques such as Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) and Attribute-Based Access Control (ABAC) cannot provide dynamic responses to context changes or intelligible access outcomes. This paper proposed mechanism, called Intelligent Access Control Mechanism with Explainable Artificial Intelligence (IACM-XAI), for e- healthcare cloud environment as per third sustainable development goal. The Deep Neural Network (DNN) based access classifier framework as proposed by us takes advantage of SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) and Local Interpretable Model-agnostic Explanations (LIME) for decision transparency, auditability and adaptability. The DNN was applied to the Medical Information Mart for Intensive Care III (MIMIC-III) Clinical Access Log data with synthetic policy data. This produced a validation accuracy of 97.4 % and a FPR of 0.96 %. Access levels function with a layered approach that makes use of both role-based access control and attribute-based access control, which may further be configured with the commitment of unnecessary features to prevent cluttering of the UI. The healthcare system deploys on AWS as a microservice using Docker and includes MongoDB Atlas and FastAPI making it simple to scale and much easier for actual integration into the healthcare space. The accuracy and interpretability of the IACM-XAI built models outperform ABAC and blockchain models. It also has a better latency performance. In the case of policy decision made with a transparent, it can reach 30 percent while latent to access could go to 40 percent. This study will contribute to the literature on the safety of AI through a new analysis. Notably, when you combine automation and AI together they enable effective decision-making within a single system along with explanation and governance of decisions. This was never attempted as far as health care system is concerned.
