TI  - Leveraging UE-Level Collaborative Intelligence for Scalable Jamming Detection in 5G Networks
AU  - J. Xu
AU  - V. Loscrì
T2  - 2025 21st International Conference on Distributed Computing in Smart Systems and the Internet of Things (DCOSS-IoT)
AB  - The proliferation of Fifth-generation ($\mathbf{5 G}$) networks enhances connectivity but also increases vulnerability to threats like jamming. Traditional detection relying on external RF hardware is costly and impractical for widespread 5G and Internet of Things (IoT) deployments. Our prior research demonstrated the feasibility of on-device jamming detection using native User Equipment (UE) log messages, which provide valuable insights but suffer from limited spatial awareness when used locally. This Work-In-Progress (WIP) paper introduces a novel, lightweight collaborative intelligence framework to achieve scalable, network-wide jamming situational awareness. We outline a system where distributed UE reports binary jamming indicators, derived from standard on-device signal quality measurements. A central or distributed fusion mechanism aggregates these simple inputs to achieve network-wide jamming detection, map the affected area, and estimate the jammer's location. We argue that this approach offers a scalable solution for enhanced network protection with minimal device complexity and communication overhead, directly overcoming the limitations of purely local detection. This paper details the proposed architecture, discusses fusion and localization algorithms tailored for binary data, outlines key operational challenges including data reliability trade-offs and system dynamics with mobile UE, and presents our experimental evaluation plan.
