TY  - CONF
TI  - A Data Driven Assessment to Predict TOC of a Shale Formation Using Machine Learning
AU  - M. I. Hossain
AU  - M. M. Ur Rashid
AU  - A. Sen Gupta
T2  - 2025 International Conference on Quantum Photonics, Artificial Intelligence, and Networking (QPAIN)
AB  - Since shale formations are becoming a more significant and dependable source of energy in the future, it is crucial for reservoir characterization and development planning to accurately anticipate Total Organic Carbon (TOC). Conventional well log interpretation and geochemical analyses are the mainstays of TOC estimate, but they can be expensive, time-consuming, and have limited predictive accuracy. Recent developments in machine learning (ML) and artificial intelligence (AI) have made data-driven approaches very promising for increasing the accuracy of TOC predictions. This study is novel in that it compares five underutilized supervised machine learning models, specifically for TOC prediction using traditional well log data: Adaptive Boosting (AdaBoost), Categorical Boosting (CatBoost), Light Gradient-Boosting Machine (LGBM), Gradient Boosting (GB), and Decision Tree (DT).This dataset includes data from shale formations for neutron gamma rays, caliper logs (CAL), acoustic logs (AC), density logs (DEN), compensated neutron logs (CNL), and resistivity logs $\left(R_{t}\right)$. Mean Absolute Error (MAE), Root Mean Square Error (RMSE), and $R$-squared ($R^{2}$) metrics were used to assess the model's performance. The outcomes show that CatBoost performed better than the other models, obtaining a $R^{2}$ value of 0.88 with little computing effort. The relative $R^{2}$ values for the AdaBoost, GB, DT, and LGBM models were 0.85, 0.86, 0.73, and 0.84 respectively. These results validate the better predictive capacity and practical usability of modern boosting algorithms in TOC estimation and real-field shale reservoir characterization.
