TI  - Statistical Monitoring of Optical Channels for Physical-Layer Security in Industrial Systems
AU  - K. J
AU  - H. B.S.
AU  - H. D
AU  - J. V.L.
T2  - 2026 International Conference on Computer Networks and Inventive Communication Technologies (ICCNCT)
AB  - Industrial control systems depend on cryptographic and network-layer protections but lack mechanisms to verify the integrity of the underlying physical communication channel. Optical links are vulnerable to obstruction, tapping, misalignment, and mechanical disturbances that bypass higher-layer security controls entirely. This paper presents the Optical Channel Integrity Monitor (OCIM), a hardware-based system that applies Statistical Process Control to continuously monitor optical intensity along a laser–photodiode path. OCIM employs adaptive baseline estimation, three-sigma deviation detection, and hysteresis-based confirmation to identify sustained physical disturbances. Upon detection, a hardware relay isolates the actuator with manual-reset-only recovery, enforcing a fail-secure response. The system incorporates real-time event logging with timestamping and non-volatile configuration storage for forensic traceability. Experimental validation demonstrates zero false positives under stable conditions and detection latency of 390–450 ms across three disturbance categories, complementing existing encryption-based security at the physical layer.
