AU  - R. S
T2  - 2026 3rd International Conference on Research Methodologies in Knowledge Management, Artificial Intelligence and Telecommunication Engineering (RMKMATE)
AB  - Hackers still use Secure Shell (SSH) services to get into systems a lot since they are common and may be located on public networks. A lot of how classic intrusion detection systems work is by looking at signatures or the network as a whole. But these strategies don't usually work after an interactive session is put up to figure out what the attacker wants to do. This paper describes a behavior-driven strategy for looking at SSH assaults using honeypot log data, with an emphasis on extracting features at the session level and showing them in real time. We gather logs of interactions from a Cowrie SSH honeypot and turn them into sessions for hackers. After that, we turn them into high-dimensional behavioral feature vectors that alter based on time, language, and purpose. A real-time dashboard has these capabilities and can tell the difference between attacks that are done by bots and attacks that are done by people. The suggested approach puts a lot of attention on being modular, easy to comprehend, and easy to see how it operates. The article also discusses a proposed machine learning enhancement that would utilize labeled attack datasets to autonomously categorize attacker behavior based on identified characteristics.
