TI  - A Hybrid Prior-Guided Framework for Magnetotelluric Inversion Constrained by Well-Log Data
AU  - Y. Yang
AU  - X. Wang
AU  - S. Wang
AU  - C. Yang
AU  - Z. Chen
AU  - P. Jiang
AU  - Y. Li
T2  - IEEE Geoscience and Remote Sensing Letters
AB  - Deep learning (DL) has become an efficient approach for magnetotelluric (MT) inversion, but its reliability depends on whether the training models represent the electrical structure of the target region. The existing workflows commonly use randomly generated models, which may poorly match regional stratigraphy and resistivity variations, whereas well logs offer direct but limited constraints on subsurface resistivity. Here, we propose a hybrid prior-guided MT inversion framework that transfers limited well-log information into data-driven MT inversion through learned structural priors. Regionally constrained 3-D training samples are constructed from well-log statistics and geological information, and the resulting network predictions are incorporated through prior-constrained and cross-gradient (CG)-constrained inversion strategies. The synthetic and field-data applications demonstrate complementary effects of the two strategies. Prior-constrained inversion improves early rms convergence, indicating that the value prior can guide the initial model update. In contrast, CG-constrained inversion provides more stable structural recovery under comparable final misfit. In the synthetic test, the CG-constrained result delineates more coherent shallow layers and intermediate-depth anomalies. In the field application, it better matches the well-log trend over 0–6 km; relative to free inversion, the log-resistivity RMSE decreases by 15.79% and the correlation coefficient increases from 0.1270 to 0.2528. These results indicate that DL predictions are more useful as structural priors in data-constrained inversion than as stand-alone inversion results, offering a practical way to incorporate limited well-log constraints into 3-D MT inversion.
