TI  - TeleGovX: Deterministic Ontology-Governed Semantic Mapping of TON_IoT Telemetry
AU  - J. Muhammad
AU  - R. Das
T2  - 2026 10th International Artificial Intelligence and Data Processing Symposium (IDAP)
AB  - Smart environments generate heterogeneous telemetry streams from diverse IoT devices, network services, and wireless infrastructure. Representing this heterogeneity in a machine-interpretable, semantically structured form remains a persistent challenge for knowledge management and intelligent city applications. This paper presents TeleGovX, a deterministic, ontology-governed semantic-mapping framework for smart-environment IoT telemetry. Operating as a three-agent pipeline of deterministic, agent-inspired components, TeleGovX maps streaming fields onto a versioned, Protégé-inspectable OWL ontology (SmartCity v0.3: 41 classes, 22 object properties, 28 datatype properties) and performs validated ABox population with a human-gated TBox proposal queue: new-term suggestions are queued for approval and never applied automatically. The framework applies dual-layer verification: a per-field structural governance predicate (datatype, domain or range, and device or observation separation) at commit time, followed by graph-level SHACL conformance checking, with a provenance-complete, hash-chained audit trail over every decision. In a five-window evaluation on four telemetry schemas, the full pipeline correctly maps 16 of 17 unique schema fields (precision 1.00, recall 0.94), against 15 of 17 (0.88 recall) for an exact-rule baseline, while admitting zero invalid triples into the accepted graph. Under controlled transfer, a schema withheld from all mapping rules is mapped at 3 of 4 fields (recall 0.75) versus 2 of 4 (0.50) for the baseline. Three fresh-process repetitions reproduce byte-identical canonical digests; the ontology shows no detected inconsistency under the HermiT reasoner; and a secondary two-corpus governance stress test exhibits fail-closed behaviour on foreign schemas.
