TY  - CONF
TI  - Comparative Analysis of Predictive and Prescriptive Process Monitoring on BPJS Data
AU  - M. Khoirullutfansyah
AU  - A. P. Kurniati
T2  - 2026 14th International Conference on Information and Communication Technology (ICoICT)
AB  - In 2024, Indonesia’s national health insurance (BPJS Kesehatan) faced an IDR 20 trillion deficit driven by catastrophic diseases, necessitating accurate cost prediction models. Various Machine Learning (ML) approaches have been widely utilized to forecast these costs; however, they are fundamentally limited by their static nature, completely ignoring the sequential context and dynamic variations of medical procedures. To overcome this shortcoming, Predictive Process Monitoring (PPM) was adopted to explicitly capture the sequential order of historical events. Yet, standard PPM possesses a critical flaw: it relies purely on probabilistic forecasting without validating whether the predicted pathway complies with logical medical protocols. To address this gap, we propose a Prescriptive Process Monitoring (PrPM) approach integrating a Multitask Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) network with a Ball Tree-based nearest neighbor search, utilizing a formally discovered process model to explicitly check and enforce the validity of the clinical pathways. Utilizing BPJS data from 2015 to 2020, we compared PrPM against a PPM baseline. Results show that PrPM significantly reduces Mean Absolute Error (MAE) in cost estimation for highly complex and long-duration cases. However, it failed to improve procedural compliance (Fitness) and severely degraded sequence structural similarity (Damerau-Levenshtein Distance) due to forced fallback deviations. We conclude that while retrieval-augmented PrPM optimizes cost predictions, it introduces critical structural distortions, highlighting a significant trade-off between financial accuracy and clinical realism.
