AB  - In recent years, Low Power Wide Area Network (LPWAN), which has realized substantial low cost and low power consumption, is attracting attention and has been put into practical use. It has enough bandwidth for general IoT services. On the other hand, on its own, it cannot handle the transmission of large amounts of data, such as collecting log data in the event of machine abnormalities because it is a narrow band and there is a limit to the amount of traffic per day. For this reason, we are studying a method that can transfer a large amount of data to the cloud without increasing communication costs and power consumption. With this method, the data (hereinafter referred to as files) is relayed to smartphones (hereinafter referred to as terminals) which are passing by near the machine, which then upload the files when they attach to a free Wi-Fi LAN, under the control of the cloud. In this paper, we show the experimental results of transferring this large amount of data, assuming an actual service, and report the necessity of file division and redundancy technology. Then, we present the problems when applying the conventional Forward Error Correcting (FEC) techniques to our system, and propose a method to solve them. Finally, we report the effectiveness of the proposed method as determined by simulations.
