AB  - Event cameras provide high temporal resolution and dynamic range, making them well-suited for stereo matching under fast motion and extreme lighting conditions, which overcomes many challenges in practical applications. However, the inherent sparsity of event data makes it challenging to directly regress accurate disparities over a wide range, posing significant challenges to learning-based methods. To address this, we draw inspiration from the hierarchical perception mechanism of the human visual system, which perceives coarse structures before refining details. We explore applying this mechanism to the regression stage and propose a novel coarse-to-fine framework. Specifically, the disparity range is discretized into multiple coarse intervals, corresponding to different disparity levels. In the coarse regression, the model distinguishes which level each pixel belongs to. In the fine regression, the prior information of coarse regression makes it much easier to perform fine regression. The bottleneck caused by the wide range of disparity is overcome, which greatly unleashes the potential of event-based stereo matching. Additionally, to ensure the accuracy of prior information provided by the coarse stage, we introduce a disparity refinement & error correction module. Extensive experiments on MVSEC and DSEC demonstrate that our method consistently outperforms state-of-the-art approaches, achieving superior performance in real-world event-based stereo matching tasks.
