TY  - CHAP
TI  - Automation, Continuous Auditing, and Advanced Analytics
AU  - Jason Edwards
T2  - Cybersecurity Auditing: Principles, Practices, and Frameworks
AB  - <p>This chapter focuses on how cybersecurity auditors assess the systems, configurations, and defenses that protect an organization's digital backbone&#x2014;its network and supporting infrastructure. It explains that auditing network security involves evaluating both design and operation, verifying that the architecture, segmentation, and controls function together to preserve confidentiality, integrity, and availability. Readers learn how to review firewalls, routers, intrusion detection systems, and endpoint protections to ensure that rules, configurations, and monitoring align with policy and risk tolerance. This chapter underscores that a network audit is not a penetration test but a governance exercise, ensuring that the organization's controls work as intended, are properly maintained, and evolve alongside new technologies and threats. The discussion explores how to audit key infrastructure components systematically. It details how to examine network topology diagrams, configuration baselines, patch management records, and vulnerability scans for completeness and accuracy. Readers gain insight into evaluating security event logging, alert correlation, and incident escalation workflows. This chapter also explains how to assess change management processes that govern network modifications and how to verify that access to administrative interfaces is restricted, monitored, and periodically reviewed. Practical guidance is given for balancing technical detail with assurance objectives&#x2014;enabling auditors to validate control effectiveness without needing to reperform operational work. In conclusion, this chapter connects network auditing to the organization's overall resilience and threat defense posture. It shows how weaknesses in infrastructure governance&#x2014;such as undocumented changes, misaligned firewall rules, or neglected firmware updates&#x2014;can create systemic risks that compromise even well&#x2010;designed policies. Readers learn how audit results inform security architecture improvements, drive configuration discipline, and enhance situational awareness across both technical and leadership levels. By mastering the audit of network security and infrastructure, cybersecurity professionals ensure that their assurance activities extend deep into the organization's core defenses, validating that technology, process, and policy work together to safeguard every connection that supports the enterprise.</p>
